PathwayData.put("data/reading_writing/units/L06U1.json",{"strand":"reading_writing","level":6,"cefr":"B1b","unit":1,"theme":"Ancient Civilizations","essential_question":"What can ancient peoples teach us today?","schema_version":"1.0","core_words":["ancient","civilization","empire","ruins","archaeologist","artifact","inscription","trade route","caravan","kingdom","monument","century","settlement","preserve"],"recycled_words":["desert","camel","trade","history","museum","past simple","first","then","finally","summary","paraphrase","report","protect"],"rubrics":[{"id":"RW-RUBRIC-5T","title":"Analytic writing rubric","traits":[{"name":"Ideas","name_ar":"الأفكار","bands":[{"score":4,"descriptor":"My ideas are clear, interesting and fully developed with strong details.","descriptor_ar":"أفكاري واضحة وممتعة ومدعومة بتفاصيل قوية."},{"score":3,"descriptor":"My ideas are clear and have enough details.","descriptor_ar":"أفكاري واضحة ومعها تفاصيل كافية."},{"score":2,"descriptor":"My main idea is there but needs more details.","descriptor_ar":"فكرتي الرئيسية موجودة لكنها تحتاج إلى تفاصيل أكثر."},{"score":1,"descriptor":"My ideas are unclear or very few.","descriptor_ar":"أفكاري غير واضحة أو قليلة جدًا."}]},{"name":"Organization","name_ar":"التنظيم","bands":[{"score":4,"descriptor":"Clear beginning, middle and end; ideas flow with linking words.","descriptor_ar":"بداية ووسط ونهاية واضحة، والأفكار مترابطة بكلمات الربط."},{"score":3,"descriptor":"Clear order with some linking words.","descriptor_ar":"ترتيب واضح مع بعض كلمات الربط."},{"score":2,"descriptor":"Some order, but parts are mixed up.","descriptor_ar":"يوجد بعض الترتيب لكن بعض الأجزاء غير مرتبة."},{"score":1,"descriptor":"Ideas are not in order.","descriptor_ar":"الأفكار غير مرتبة."}]},{"name":"Vocabulary","name_ar":"المفردات","bands":[{"score":4,"descriptor":"I use exact, varied words, including new unit words, correctly.","descriptor_ar":"أستخدم كلمات دقيقة ومتنوعة، منها كلمات الوحدة الجديدة، بشكل صحيح."},{"score":3,"descriptor":"I use some new words correctly.","descriptor_ar":"أستخدم بعض الكلمات الجديدة بشكل صحيح."},{"score":2,"descriptor":"I use simple words; some are repeated or wrong.","descriptor_ar":"أستخدم كلمات بسيطة، وبعضها مكرر أو غير صحيح."},{"score":1,"descriptor":"I use very few words; the meaning is hard to understand.","descriptor_ar":"أستخدم كلمات قليلة جدًا ويصعب فهم المعنى."}]},{"name":"Sentence fluency","name_ar":"سلاسة الجمل","bands":[{"score":4,"descriptor":"My sentences are complete, varied in length and easy to read aloud.","descriptor_ar":"جملي كاملة ومتنوعة الطول وسهلة القراءة بصوت عالٍ."},{"score":3,"descriptor":"Most sentences are complete, with some variety.","descriptor_ar":"معظم جملي كاملة مع بعض التنويع."},{"score":2,"descriptor":"Many sentences are short or start the same way.","descriptor_ar":"كثير من جملي قصيرة أو تبدأ بالطريقة نفسها."},{"score":1,"descriptor":"Sentences are incomplete or run together.","descriptor_ar":"جملي ناقصة أو متداخلة."}]},{"name":"Conventions","name_ar":"قواعد الكتابة","bands":[{"score":4,"descriptor":"Capitals, punctuation, spelling and grammar are almost always correct.","descriptor_ar":"الحروف الكبيرة وعلامات الترقيم والإملاء والقواعد صحيحة دائمًا تقريبًا."},{"score":3,"descriptor":"A few mistakes that do not affect meaning.","descriptor_ar":"أخطاء قليلة لا تؤثر في المعنى."},{"score":2,"descriptor":"Several mistakes; the meaning is sometimes unclear.","descriptor_ar":"عدة أخطاء، والمعنى أحيانًا غير واضح."},{"score":1,"descriptor":"Many mistakes make it hard to read.","descriptor_ar":"أخطاء كثيرة تجعل القراءة صعبة."}]}]}],"lessons":[{"id":"RW-L06-U1-LS1","title":"Word Power: Ancient Civilizations","lesson_type":"vocabulary","objectives":["read, say and use 14 words about ancient civilizations and 4 Tier 2 words (evidence, era, establish, decline)","use Greek and Latin roots (archae = ancient, chron = time, logy = study of, script = write, cent = hundred) to work out the meaning of new words","break a long word into root, prefix and suffix and explain its meaning"],"objectives_ar":["قراءة ونطق واستخدام 14 كلمة عن الحضارات القديمة و4 كلمات أكاديمية.","استخدام الجذور الإغريقية واللاتينية لاستنتاج معاني الكلمات الجديدة.","تقسيم الكلمة الطويلة إلى جذر وسابقة ولاحقة وشرح معناها."],"success_criteria":["I can use the ancient-civilization words correctly in sentences.","I can name the meaning of the roots archae, chron, logy, script and cent.","I can work out the meaning of a new word such as *chronology* or *manuscript* from its parts."],"success_criteria_ar":["أستطيع استخدام كلمات الحضارات القديمة في جمل بشكل صحيح.","أستطيع ذكر معاني الجذور archae وchron وlogy وscript وcent.","أستطيع استنتاج معنى كلمة جديدة مثل chronology أو manuscript من أجزائها."],"map_area":"Vocabulary: Acquisition and Use","vocabulary":[{"word":"ancient","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈeɪnʃənt/","definition":"very old; from a time thousands of years ago","arabic":"قديم / عتيق","example":"Hegra is an ancient city in AlUla.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V01","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V01","tier":"core"},{"word":"civilization","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌsɪvələˈzeɪʃən/","definition":"a large, organized society with its own cities, laws, writing and culture","arabic":"حضارة","example":"The Nabataean civilization built cities in rock.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V02","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V02","tier":"core"},{"word":"empire","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈɛmˌpaɪər/","definition":"a group of countries or lands ruled by one powerful ruler or government","arabic":"إمبراطورية","example":"The Roman Empire was very large.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V03","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V03","tier":"core"},{"word":"ruins","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈruɪnz/","definition":"the parts of old buildings that are left after they have fallen down","arabic":"أطلال / آثار","example":"We walked among the ruins of an old fort.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V04","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V04","tier":"core"},{"word":"archaeologist","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌɑrkiˈɑlədʒɪst/","definition":"a scientist who studies ancient people by digging up and examining what they left","arabic":"عالم آثار","example":"The archaeologist found pottery in the sand.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V05","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V05","tier":"core"},{"word":"artifact","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈɑrtəˌfækt/","definition":"an object made by people long ago, such as a tool, a coin or a pot","arabic":"قطعة أثرية","example":"The museum shows artifacts from Tayma.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V06","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V06","tier":"core"},{"word":"inscription","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ɪnˈskrɪpʃən/","definition":"words carved or written on stone, metal or another hard surface","arabic":"نقش","example":"The inscription above the tomb gives the owner's name.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V07","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V07","tier":"core"},{"word":"trade route","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈtreɪd ˌrut/","definition":"a path that traders use to carry goods from one place to another","arabic":"طريق تجاري","example":"Incense traveled along the trade route.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V08","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V08","tier":"core"},{"word":"caravan","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈkærəˌvæn/","definition":"a group of people and animals, often camels, traveling together","arabic":"قافلة","example":"The caravan crossed the desert at night.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V09","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V09","tier":"core"},{"word":"kingdom","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈkɪŋdəm/","definition":"a country or area ruled by a king or a queen","arabic":"مملكة","example":"Dedan was the center of an ancient kingdom.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V10","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V10","tier":"core"},{"word":"monument","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈmɑnjəmənt/","definition":"a large, old or important building or structure that people remember","arabic":"معلم أثري / نُصب","example":"Qasr al-Farid is a famous monument.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V11","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V11","tier":"core"},{"word":"century","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈsɛntʃəri/","definition":"a period of one hundred years","arabic":"قرن","example":"The tomb is about twenty centuries old.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V12","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V12","tier":"core"},{"word":"settlement","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈsɛtəlmənt/","definition":"a place where people come to live and build homes","arabic":"مستوطنة / تجمّع سكني","example":"Archaeologists found an early settlement near the oasis.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V13","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V13","tier":"core"},{"word":"preserve","pos":"verb","ipa":"/prɪˈzɝv/","definition":"to keep something safe so that it stays in good condition","arabic":"يحافظ على / يصون","example":"Dry desert air helped preserve the tombs.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V14","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V14","tier":"core"},{"word":"evidence","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈɛvədəns/","definition":"facts or objects that show that something is true","arabic":"دليل","example":"Coins are evidence of ancient trade.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V15","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V15","tier":"tier2"},{"word":"era","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈɪrə/","definition":"a long period of time in history with special features","arabic":"حقبة / عصر","example":"The Nabataean era lasted for centuries.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V16","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V16","tier":"tier2"},{"word":"establish","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ɪˈstæblɪʃ/","definition":"to start a city, a group or a system that lasts a long time","arabic":"يؤسّس","example":"Traders established a market near the wells.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V17","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V17","tier":"tier2"},{"word":"decline","pos":"verb / noun","ipa":"/dɪˈklaɪn/","definition":"to become smaller, weaker or less important; a slow fall","arabic":"يتراجع / تراجع","example":"The city declined when trade moved to the sea.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V18","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V18","tier":"tier2"}],"explanation":{"text":"Many English words – especially in science and history – are built from **Greek and Latin roots**. A **root** is the main part of a word that carries its meaning. If you know the root, you can **work out** the meaning of many new words.\n\nKey roots: **archae / archaeo** (Greek) = **ancient** → *archaeology, archaeologist*; **chron** (Greek) = **time** → *chronological, chronicle, synchronize*; **logy / ologist** (Greek) = **the study of / a person who studies** → *geology, biologist*; **script / scrib** (Latin) = **write** → *inscription, manuscript, describe*; **cent** (Latin) = **hundred** → *century, percent, centimeter*.\n\nSteps: (1) **break** the word into parts: *in + script + ion*; (2) give the **meaning** of each part: *on/into + write + thing*; (3) **put the meanings together**: *words written (carved) into stone*; (4) **check** with the sentence.","examples":["**archae** + **ologist** → *archaeologist* = a person who studies ancient things.","**chron** + **o** + **logical** → *chronological* = in time order.","**chron** + **icle** → *chronicle* = a record of events in time order.","**syn** (together) + **chron** + **ize** → *synchronize* = make things happen at the same time.","**geo** (earth) + **logy** → *geology* = the study of rocks and the earth.","**in** + **script** + **ion** → *inscription* = words carved into stone.","**manu** (hand) + **script** → *manuscript* = a book or text written by hand.","**cent** + **ury** → *century* = one hundred years · **per cent** → out of every hundred.","In context: *The archaeologists arranged the artifacts in **chronological** order, from the oldest to the newest.*"],"common_errors":["✗ An archaeologist studies stars. → ✓ An archaeologist studies ancient people and objects. (archae = ancient.)","✗ Chronological means in size order. → ✓ Chronological means in time order. (chron = time.)","✗ A century is ten years. → ✓ A century is one hundred years. (cent = hundred.)","✗ The inscription is a kind of tomb. → ✓ The inscription is the writing carved on the tomb. (script = write.)","✗ The tomb is very ancient old. → ✓ The tomb is very ancient. (ancient already means very old.)","✗ Archaeologists founded coins. → ✓ Archaeologists found coins. (find → **found**; *founded* = established.)","✗ Guessing a root word from one letter. → ✓ Break the whole word into parts and check with the sentence. (Check in context.)"],"arabic":"كثير من الكلمات الإنجليزية، ولا سيما في العلوم والتاريخ، مبنيّة على جذور إغريقية ولاتينية، والجذر هو الجزء الذي يحمل المعنى الأساسي.\n\nجذور مهمة: archae = قديم، وchron = زمن، وlogy = علم/دراسة، وscript = كتابة، وcent = مئة.\n\nالخطوات: قسّم الكلمة إلى أجزاء، وأعط معنى كل جزء، واجمع المعاني، ثم تحقّق من السياق.\n\nانتبه: found ماضي find، أما founded فمعناها «أسّس»."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:30","narration":"Two thousand years ago, a city was carved out of solid rock in the Arabian desert. Today, we travel back in time!","on_screen":"Ancient Civilizations 🏛️","visual":"Sunrise over the sandstone tombs of Hegra in AlUla."},{"time":"00:30–01:30","narration":"Ancient, civilization, empire, ruins, archaeologist, artifact, inscription, trade route, caravan, kingdom, monument, century, settlement, preserve!","on_screen":"ancient · civilization · empire · ruins · archaeologist · artifact · inscription · trade route · caravan · kingdom · monument · century · settlement · preserve","visual":"Each word appears carved on a stone tablet with a picture."},{"time":"01:30–02:05","narration":"Archaeologists look for evidence from every era: who established a city, and why did it decline?","on_screen":"evidence · era · establish · decline","visual":"A timeline with a rising and falling line over a city."},{"time":"02:05–03:30","narration":"Word detectives! Archae means ancient. Chron means time. Logy means study of. Script means write. Cent means hundred.","on_screen":"archae · chron · logy · script · cent","visual":"Words split into colored puzzle pieces and join again."},{"time":"03:30–04:00","narration":"Break the word, find the roots, join the meanings – and check the sentence!","on_screen":"Break → Meaning → Join → Check","visual":"A magnifying glass reveals the parts of 'inscription'."}],"captions_ar":["قبل ألفي عام نُحتت مدينة في الصخر الصلب في صحراء الجزيرة العربية. اليوم نعود بالزمن إلى الوراء!","قديم، حضارة، إمبراطورية، أطلال، عالم آثار، قطعة أثرية، نقش، طريق تجاري، قافلة، مملكة، معلم أثري، قرن، مستوطنة، يحافظ على!","يبحث علماء الآثار عن الأدلة من كل حقبة: من أسّس المدينة، ولماذا تراجعت؟","محققو الكلمات! archae تعني قديم، وchron تعني زمن، وlogy تعني دراسة، وscript تعني كتابة، وcent تعني مئة.","قسّم الكلمة، وجِد الجذور، واجمع المعاني، ثم تحقّق من الجملة!"],"voice":"Narrator: American English, warm documentary-style male voice, speed 1.0."},"texts":[],"activities":[{"id":"RW-L06-U1-LS1-A1","type":"mcq","stage":"controlled","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correct word.","instructions_ar":"اختر الكلمة الصحيحة.","items":[{"q":"The ___ carefully brushed the sand away from the old clay pot.","answer":"archaeologist","explanation":"Archaeologist.","explanation_ar":"عالمة الآثار.","options":["archaeologist","caravan","empire","century"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"A line of fifty camels carrying incense is a ___.","answer":"caravan","explanation":"Caravan.","explanation_ar":"قافلة.","options":["monument","settlement","ruin","caravan"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"The words carved above the tomb door are called an ___.","answer":"inscription","explanation":"Inscription.","explanation_ar":"نقش.","options":["empire","era","artifact","inscription"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Coins, lamps and tools found in an ancient city are ___.","answer":"artifacts","explanation":"Artifact.","explanation_ar":"قطع أثرية.","options":["kingdoms","centuries","artifacts","caravans"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Only a few broken walls of the old fort are left. These are its ___.","answer":"ruins","explanation":"Ruins.","explanation_ar":"أطلاله.","options":["monuments","routes","ruins","eras"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"The first families built mud houses beside the spring and started a small ___.","answer":"settlement","explanation":"Settlement.","explanation_ar":"تجمّعًا سكنيًا.","options":["empire","settlement","inscription","century"],"dok":2,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U1-LS1-A2","type":"mcq","stage":"guided","instructions":"Guided practice: Greek and Latin roots. Choose.","instructions_ar":"الجذور الإغريقية واللاتينية: اختر.","items":[{"q":"The root *chron* in *chronological* means ___.","answer":"time","explanation":"chron = time.","explanation_ar":"زمن.","options":["hand","earth","time","write"],"dok":1,"skill":"roots","stage":"guided"},{"q":"The root *archae* in *archaeology* means ___.","answer":"ancient","explanation":"archae = ancient.","explanation_ar":"قديم.","options":["hundred","ancient","life","together"],"dok":1,"skill":"roots","stage":"guided"},{"q":"What does a *geologist* study, if *geo* means earth?","answer":"rocks and the earth","explanation":"geo + logist.","explanation_ar":"الصخور والأرض.","options":["rocks and the earth","ancient coins","stars","animals"],"dok":2,"skill":"roots","stage":"guided"},{"q":"A museum shows a *manuscript* from Makkah. *Manu* means hand and *script* means write. What is it?","answer":"a book or text written by hand","explanation":"manu + script.","explanation_ar":"مخطوطة مكتوبة باليد.","options":["a stone statue","a map of a trade route","a printed newspaper","a book or text written by hand"],"dok":2,"skill":"roots","stage":"guided"},{"q":"If a monument is *five centuries* old, how old is it?","answer":"500 years","explanation":"cent = hundred.","explanation_ar":"500 سنة.","options":["500 years","50 years","5 years","5,000 years"],"dok":2,"skill":"roots","stage":"guided"},{"q":"The teacher asked us to *synchronize* our watches before the museum trip. *Syn* means together. What does she want?","answer":"all our watches showing the same time","explanation":"syn + chron.","explanation_ar":"أن تُظهر الساعات الوقت نفسه.","options":["new watches","all our watches showing the same time","watches with inscriptions","no watches at all"],"dok":3,"skill":"roots","stage":"guided"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U1-LS1-A3","type":"gap_fill","stage":"freer","instructions":"Freer practice: Use the word bank. Each word fits one sentence.","instructions_ar":"استخدم بنك الكلمات.","items":[{"q":"Old coins found far from the sea are ________ that the city traded with distant lands.","answer":"evidence","explanation":"Proof.","explanation_ar":"دليل.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"The Nabataean ________ lasted for several centuries.","answer":"era","explanation":"Period.","explanation_ar":"حقبة.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Traders ________ a market beside the wells, and soon a town grew there.","answer":"established","explanation":"Started.","explanation_ar":"أسّسوا.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"When ships began to carry goods by sea, the desert city started to ________.","answer":"decline","explanation":"Become weaker.","explanation_ar":"تتراجع.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"The archaeologists put the artifacts in ________ order, from oldest to newest.","answer":"chronological","explanation":"Time order.","explanation_ar":"زمني.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"The dry desert air helped ________ the carved tombs for two thousand years.","answer":"preserve","explanation":"Keep safe.","explanation_ar":"يحافظ على.","stage":"freer"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U1-LS1-A4","type":"open","stage":"production","instructions":"Writing: Write with the new words.","instructions_ar":"اكتب باستخدام الكلمات الجديدة.","items":[{"q":"Break *inscription* into parts and explain its meaning.","answer":"in (into/on) + script (write) + ion (thing) = words written or carved into stone.","explanation":"Word parts.","explanation_ar":"أجزاء الكلمة.","dok":2,"skill":"roots","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write two sentences about an ancient place in Saudi Arabia using *ancient* and *preserve*.","answer":"Hegra is an ancient city in AlUla. Saudi Arabia works hard to preserve its tombs for the future.","explanation":"Use the words.","explanation_ar":"استخدم الكلمات.","dok":2,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"production"},{"q":"What evidence could tell an archaeologist that people once traded in a place?","answer":"Coins from other countries, pottery made far away and inscriptions that mention traders could be evidence of trade.","explanation":"Reasoning.","explanation_ar":"استدلال.","dok":3,"skill":"reasoning","stage":"production"},{"q":"Which artifact from your home might an archaeologist find in 2,000 years? What would it show about us?","answer":"An archaeologist might find my tablet. It would show that we used digital devices to learn and communicate.","explanation":"Imagine.","explanation_ar":"تخيّل.","dok":3,"skill":"connect","stage":"production"}]}],"game":{"name":"Root Archaeologists","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["On this screen: answer the lesson questions as fast as you can.","In class: Virtual dig: the child brushes sand off word parts, drags them together to build a word and chooses its meaning. Each correct word adds an artifact to the child's museum shelf.","Teams take turns 'digging' two or three cards from the tray.","They try to build a real word (e.g., chron + icle).","They explain the meaning from the parts and check it with a meaning card.","If they cannot build a word, the cards go back into the sand.","Correct word = 2 points; correct meaning from the parts = 1 point."],"data":[]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"Which word means *very old; from thousands of years ago*?","answer":"ancient","explanation":"Ancient.","explanation_ar":"قديم.","options":["modern","ancient","digital","recent"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary"},{"q":"What is a *root*?","answer":"the main part of a word that carries its meaning","explanation":"Definition.","explanation_ar":"الجذر.","options":["the last letter of a word","a word that rhymes","a kind of sentence","the main part of a word that carries its meaning"],"dok":1,"skill":"roots"},{"q":"*The town **declined** after the trade route moved to the sea.* *Declined* means ___.","answer":"became weaker and less important","explanation":"Decline.","explanation_ar":"تراجعت.","options":["grew bigger","became weaker and less important","built a wall","got a new king"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary in context"},{"q":"Which word has a root that means *time*?","answer":"chronicle","explanation":"chron = time.","explanation_ar":"chronicle.","options":["geology","manuscript","chronicle","century"],"dok":1,"skill":"roots"},{"q":"Which sentence about the dig is correct?","answer":"The archaeologists found a bronze lamp near the tomb.","explanation":"find → found.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["The archaeologists founded a bronze lamp near the tomb.","The archaeologists found a bronze lamp near the tomb.","The archaeologists finded a bronze lamp near the tomb.","The archaeologists find a bronze lamp yesterday."],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar"},{"q":"What does *biology* mean if *bio* means life?","answer":"the study of living things","explanation":"bio + logy.","explanation_ar":"علم الأحياء.","options":["the study of rocks","writing by hand","a period of 100 years","the study of living things"],"dok":2,"skill":"roots"},{"q":"*Twenty percent of the artifacts were coins.* What does *percent* mean?","answer":"out of every hundred","explanation":"cent = hundred.","explanation_ar":"من كل مئة.","options":["out of every ten","very old","out of every hundred","carved in stone"],"dok":2,"skill":"roots"},{"q":"Which word BEST completes the sentence? *Qasr al-Farid is a huge ___ carved from one rock.*","answer":"monument","explanation":"Context.","explanation_ar":"معلم أثري.","options":["monument","caravan","settlement","evidence"],"dok":2,"skill":"vocabulary"},{"q":"A *chronometer* is a tool. Using the roots, what does it probably measure?","answer":"time","explanation":"chron + meter.","explanation_ar":"الزمن.","options":["earth","weight of coins","time","length of inscriptions"],"dok":3,"skill":"roots"},{"q":"Why are Greek and Latin roots useful when you read history and science texts?","answer":"Many long words in these subjects are built from them, so you can work out their meanings.","explanation":"Strategy.","explanation_ar":"تساعد على فهم الكلمات الطويلة.","options":["Many long words in these subjects are built from them, so you can work out their meanings.","They make texts shorter.","They are only used in poems.","They help you skip difficult words."],"dok":3,"skill":"word strategies"}],"images":[{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V01","prompt":"Picture for the word 'ancient': old stone tombs in the desert"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V02","prompt":"Picture for the word 'civilization': an ancient city with people, markets and buildings"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V03","prompt":"Picture for the word 'empire': a map with a large colored area"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V04","prompt":"Picture for the word 'ruins': broken stone walls and columns"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V05","prompt":"Picture for the word 'archaeologist': a scientist brushing sand from a pot"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V06","prompt":"Picture for the word 'artifact': an old coin, a clay lamp and a bronze tool"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V07","prompt":"Picture for the word 'inscription': ancient letters carved above a doorway"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V08","prompt":"Picture for the word 'trade route': a map with a dotted path across Arabia"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V09","prompt":"Picture for the word 'caravan': a line of camels carrying bags"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V10","prompt":"Picture for the word 'kingdom': a crown above a small map"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V11","prompt":"Picture for the word 'monument': a huge tomb carved into a single rock"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V12","prompt":"Picture for the word 'century': the number 100 on a calendar"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V13","prompt":"Picture for the word 'settlement': a few mud houses near palm trees"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V14","prompt":"Picture for the word 'preserve': workers carefully cleaning an old wall"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V15","prompt":"Picture for the word 'evidence': a magnifying glass over an old coin"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V16","prompt":"Picture for the word 'era': a timeline with a colored section"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V17","prompt":"Picture for the word 'establish': people building the first houses of a town"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V18","prompt":"Picture for the word 'decline': a line on a graph going down"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS1-01","prompt":"Ancient tombs of Hegra with a caravan and an archaeologist."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS1-02","prompt":"Icons for ancient-civilization words."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS1-03","prompt":"Word-part puzzles for roots."}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V01","script":"ancient. Hegra is an ancient city in AlUla."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V02","script":"civilization. The Nabataean civilization built cities in rock."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V03","script":"empire. The Roman Empire was very large."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V04","script":"ruins. We walked among the ruins of an old fort."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V05","script":"archaeologist. The archaeologist found pottery in the sand."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V06","script":"artifact. The museum shows artifacts from Tayma."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V07","script":"inscription. The inscription above the tomb gives the owner's name."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V08","script":"trade route. Incense traveled along the trade route."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V09","script":"caravan. The caravan crossed the desert at night."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V10","script":"kingdom. Dedan was the center of an ancient kingdom."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V11","script":"monument. Qasr al-Farid is a famous monument."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V12","script":"century. The tomb is about twenty centuries old."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V13","script":"settlement. Archaeologists found an early settlement near the oasis."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V14","script":"preserve. Dry desert air helped preserve the tombs."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V15","script":"evidence. Coins are evidence of ancient trade."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V16","script":"era. The Nabataean era lasted for centuries."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V17","script":"establish. Traders established a market near the wells."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS1-V18","script":"decline. The city declined when trade moved to the sea."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS1-VID","script":"Two thousand years ago, a city was carved out of solid rock in the Arabian desert. Today, we travel back in time! Ancient, civilization, empire, ruins, archaeologist, artifact, inscription, trade route, caravan, kingdom, monument, century, settlement, preserve! Archaeologists look for evidence from every era: who established a city, and why did it decline? Word detectives! Archae means ancient. Chron means time. Logy means study of. Script means write. Cent means hundred. Break the word, find the roots, join the meanings – and check the sentence!"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U1-LS2","title":"Hegra: The City Carved in Stone","lesson_type":"close_reading","objectives":["read a history article of about 490 words about the ancient city of Hegra","link the author's claims to the evidence that supports them (inscriptions, tombs, wells, location)","explain how archaeologists know about the past and why Hegra rose and declined"],"objectives_ar":["قراءة مقال تاريخي من نحو 490 كلمة عن مدينة الحِجر القديمة.","ربط ادعاءات الكاتب بالأدلة التي تدعمها (النقوش والمقابر والآبار والموقع).","شرح كيف يعرف علماء الآثار الماضي ولماذا ازدهرت الحِجر ثم تراجعت."],"success_criteria":["I can find the evidence for a claim in the article and quote it.","I can name four kinds of evidence archaeologists use and what each one tells them.","I can explain why Hegra grew rich and why it declined."],"success_criteria_ar":["أستطيع إيجاد الدليل على ادعاء في المقال واقتباسه.","أستطيع ذكر أربعة أنواع من الأدلة التي يستخدمها علماء الآثار وما يخبرهم به كل نوع.","أستطيع شرح سبب ثراء الحِجر وسبب تراجعها."],"map_area":"Informational Text: Key Ideas and Details","vocabulary":[{"word":"tomb","pos":"noun","ipa":"/tum/","definition":"a large grave or a stone room where a dead person is buried","arabic":"مقبرة / ضريح","example":"The tomb was carved into a sandstone rock.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS2-V01","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS2-V01","tier":"core"},{"word":"carve","pos":"verb","ipa":"/kɑrv/","definition":"to cut a hard material, such as stone or wood, to make a shape","arabic":"ينحت","example":"Workers carved the tombs by hand.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS2-V02","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS2-V02","tier":"core"},{"word":"incense","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈɪnˌsɛns/","definition":"a material that gives a sweet smell when it is burned","arabic":"بخور","example":"Caravans carried incense across Arabia.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS2-V03","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS2-V03","tier":"core"},{"word":"resin","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈrɛzɪn/","definition":"a thick, sticky liquid from trees that becomes hard","arabic":"صمغ / راتنج","example":"Frankincense is a resin from a tree.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS2-V04","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS2-V04","tier":"core"},{"word":"tax","pos":"noun","ipa":"/tæks/","definition":"money that people must pay to a government or a ruler","arabic":"ضريبة","example":"Caravans paid taxes in Hegra.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS2-V05","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS2-V05","tier":"core"},{"word":"fine","pos":"noun","ipa":"/faɪn/","definition":"money you must pay as a punishment for breaking a rule","arabic":"غرامة","example":"The inscription lists a fine for misusing the tomb.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS2-V06","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS2-V06","tier":"core"},{"word":"script","pos":"noun","ipa":"/skrɪpt/","definition":"a system of letters used for writing a language","arabic":"خطّ / أبجدية","example":"The Nabataean script developed into Arabic.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS2-V07","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS2-V07","tier":"core"}],"explanation":{"text":"History writers make **claims** – statements about the past, such as *The Nabataeans grew rich from trade*. Good writers support each claim with **evidence**: facts, objects or writing that **show** it is true. As a reader, always ask: **What is the claim? How do we know?**\n\nArchaeologists use four main kinds of evidence: **written evidence** (inscriptions, letters, coins with words); **buildings** (tombs, walls, wells); **artifacts** (pots, tools, jewelry); and **location** (where a place is – on a trade route, near water).\n\nTo link a claim and its evidence: (1) **find** the claim; (2) **look nearby** for words like *because, so, This tells…, The answer is…*; (3) **quote** or paraphrase the evidence; (4) **explain** how the evidence proves the claim.","examples":["Claim: *The Nabataeans grew rich.* → Evidence (location): *Hegra stood on this incense route, so caravans stopped there to … pay taxes.*","Claim: *The Nabataeans were experts at finding water.* → Evidence (buildings): *they cut dozens of wells deep into the rock.*","Claim: *Scientists know exactly when many tombs were carved.* → Evidence (written): *inscriptions … give the year it was made.*","Claim: *Workers carved from the top down.* → Evidence (buildings): *Its upper part is finished, but its lower part was never completed.*","Signal words: *The answer is trade.* / *This tells archaeologists that…* / *Because of these dates…*","Explaining the link: *The lower part of Qasr al-Farid is unfinished, which shows that the workers started at the top.*","Cause of the decline: *more goods traveled by ship across the Red Sea, and fewer caravans crossed the desert.*","Why the tombs survived: *the dry desert air preserved its tombs remarkably well.*","A claim WITHOUT evidence in the text ✗: *The Nabataeans loved music.* – the article gives no evidence for this."],"common_errors":["✗ Giving the claim again as the evidence. → ✓ Find a fact, an object or writing that SHOWS the claim. (Claim ≠ evidence.)","✗ Using your own ideas as evidence. → ✓ Use evidence from the text. (Stay with the text.)","✗ Copying a whole paragraph as evidence. → ✓ Quote the exact sentence or phrase that proves the claim. (Short and exact.)","✗ The Nabataeans was traders. → ✓ The Nabataeans were traders. (Plural → **were**.)","✗ Hegra declined because of it was old. → ✓ Hegra declined because fewer caravans crossed the desert. (because + subject + verb.)","✗ Mixing up Hegra and Petra. → ✓ Petra (Jordan) was the capital; Hegra was the main city in the south. (Check the facts.)","✗ Archaeologists guessed the dates. → ✓ Archaeologists read the dates in the inscriptions. (Evidence, not guessing.)"],"arabic":"يطرح كتّاب التاريخ ادعاءات عن الماضي، والكاتب الجيد يدعم كل ادعاء بدليل يثبت صحته. فاسأل دائمًا: ما الادعاء؟ وكيف نعرف ذلك؟\n\nيستخدم علماء الآثار أربعة أنواع من الأدلة: الأدلة المكتوبة (النقوش)، والمباني (المقابر والآبار)، والقطع الأثرية (الأواني والأدوات)، والموقع (على طريق تجاري أو قرب الماء).\n\nلربط الادعاء بالدليل: جِد الادعاء، وابحث قريبًا منه عن كلمات مثل because وso وThis tells، ثم اقتبس الدليل واشرح كيف يثبت الادعاء.\n\nانتبه: كانت البتراء (في الأردن) العاصمة، أما الحِجر فكانت أهم مدن الأنباط في الجنوب."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:30","narration":"Giant rocks rise from the sand like ships on a golden sea… Welcome to Hegra!","on_screen":"Hegra: The City Carved in Stone 🏜️","visual":"Drone view of sandstone outcrops and carved tombs near AlUla."},{"time":"00:30–01:30","narration":"Camel caravans carried frankincense and myrrh north. Hegra stood on the incense route…","on_screen":"Rich from Incense","visual":"An animated map: caravans move from southern Arabia through Hegra to the Mediterranean."},{"time":"01:30–02:30","narration":"Wells cut deep into rock… inscriptions above the doors… a tomb left unfinished at the bottom.","on_screen":"Wells · Inscriptions · Qasr al-Farid","visual":"Close-ups of a rock-cut well, Nabataean letters and the Lonely Castle."},{"time":"02:30–03:20","narration":"Claim: workers carved from the top down. Evidence: the lower part was never finished!","on_screen":"CLAIM ⟶ EVIDENCE","visual":"Arrows link claims to photos of the evidence."},{"time":"03:20–04:00","narration":"Ships replaced caravans, and Hegra declined – but the dry air kept its secrets safe.","on_screen":"Decline · Discovery · 2008 UNESCO","visual":"Ships sail on the Red Sea; then modern visitors walk among the tombs."}],"captions_ar":["صخور عملاقة ترتفع من الرمال كسفن في بحر ذهبي… مرحبًا بك في الحِجر!","حملت قوافل الجمال اللبان والمرّ شمالًا، وكانت الحِجر على طريق البخور…","آبار محفورة عميقًا في الصخر… نقوش فوق الأبواب… ومقبرة لم يكتمل جزؤها السفلي.","الادعاء: نحت العمال من الأعلى إلى الأسفل. الدليل: الجزء السفلي لم يكتمل!","حلّت السفن محل القوافل فتراجعت الحِجر، لكن الهواء الجاف حفظ أسرارها."],"voice":"Narrator: American English, warm documentary-style female voice, speed 1.0."},"texts":[{"type":"reading","title":"Hegra: The City Carved in Stone","body":"In the desert near AlUla, in northwest Saudi Arabia, giant sandstone rocks rise out of the sand like ships on a golden sea. Carved into these rocks are more than one hundred great tombs, some of them many times taller than a person. This is Hegra, also known as Mada'in Salih – an ancient city built by a people called the Nabataeans about two thousand years ago.\n\n**Who Were the Nabataeans?**\n\nThe Nabataeans were Arab traders who built a powerful kingdom across parts of what are today Jordan, southern Syria and northwest Saudi Arabia. Their capital was Petra, in Jordan, and Hegra was their most important city in the south. From about the first century BCE to the first century CE, Hegra was a busy stop for caravans.\n\n**Rich from Incense**\n\nWhy did a city grow in the middle of the desert? The answer is trade. Camel caravans carried frankincense and myrrh – sweet-smelling tree resins from southern Arabia – north to Egypt, Syria and the lands around the Mediterranean Sea. People used them in medicine and perfume, and they were worth a fortune. Hegra stood on this incense route, so caravans stopped there to rest, buy water and pay taxes. The Nabataeans grew rich by protecting and serving these travelers.\n\n**Masters of Water**\n\nLiving in the desert meant finding water, and the Nabataeans were experts at it. At Hegra, they cut dozens of wells deep into the rock to reach water under the ground, and they carefully collected the rare rain. This skill allowed people and camels to live where few others could survive.\n\n**What the Tombs Tell Us**\n\nThe Nabataeans did not leave many books, but they left something just as useful: their tombs. Archaeologists have counted 111 monumental tombs at Hegra. Above many of the doors is an inscription in the Nabataean script. These inscriptions name the owner of the tomb, give the year it was made and even list the fines people would pay for using it without permission. Because of these dates, scientists know exactly when many tombs were carved. Interestingly, the Nabataean script later developed into the Arabic alphabet.\n\nThe most famous tomb is Qasr al-Farid, which means \"the Lonely Castle,\" because it stands alone, carved from a single huge rock. Its upper part is finished, but its lower part was never completed. This tells archaeologists that workers carved the tombs from the top down.\n\n**Decline and Discovery**\n\nIn 106 CE, the Roman Empire took control of the Nabataean kingdom. Over the following centuries, more goods traveled by ship across the Red Sea, and fewer caravans crossed the desert. Slowly, Hegra declined and was left to the sand and the wind. However, the dry desert air preserved its tombs remarkably well.\n\nIn 2008, Hegra became Saudi Arabia's first UNESCO World Heritage Site. Today, visitors from around the world come to AlUla to walk among the tombs and imagine the caravans that once filled the city with the smell of incense.","word_count":493,"audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS2-T1","id":"TXT-RW-L06-U1-LS2-1","genre":"informational","role":"main","difficulty":"700L–850L","glossary":[{"word":"frankincense and myrrh","definition":"sweet-smelling resins from trees that grow in southern Arabia and East Africa","arabic":"اللبان والمرّ"},{"word":"BCE / CE","definition":"before the Common Era / the Common Era – used for dates before and after year 1","arabic":"قبل الميلاد / بعد الميلاد"},{"word":"UNESCO World Heritage Site","definition":"a place that the United Nations lists as important for all people to protect","arabic":"موقع تراث عالمي (يونسكو)"}]}],"activities":[{"id":"RW-L06-U1-LS2-A1","type":"mcq","stage":"controlled","instructions":"Controlled practice: Read the article. Choose the answer.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ المقال واختر الإجابة.","items":[{"q":"Where is Hegra?","answer":"near AlUla in northwest Saudi Arabia","explanation":"Paragraph 1.","explanation_ar":"قرب العُلا شمال غرب المملكة.","options":["near Riyadh in central Saudi Arabia","in Petra, Jordan","on the Red Sea coast near Jeddah","near AlUla in northwest Saudi Arabia"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What was the capital of the Nabataean kingdom?","answer":"Petra","explanation":"Who Were the Nabataeans?","explanation_ar":"البتراء.","options":["Hegra","Dedan","Rome","Petra"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What did camel caravans carry through Hegra?","answer":"frankincense and myrrh","explanation":"Rich from Incense.","explanation_ar":"اللبان والمرّ.","options":["frankincense and myrrh","gold and silver only","rice and wheat","silk and paper"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"How many monumental tombs have archaeologists counted at Hegra?","answer":"111","explanation":"What the Tombs Tell Us.","explanation_ar":"111.","options":["111","11","1,100","about 50"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What does *Qasr al-Farid* mean?","answer":"the Lonely Castle","explanation":"Paragraph 9.","explanation_ar":"القصر الفريد/الوحيد.","options":["the Golden City","the King's Well","the Lonely Castle","the Incense Road"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"When did Hegra become Saudi Arabia's first UNESCO World Heritage Site?","answer":"in 2008","explanation":"Last paragraph.","explanation_ar":"عام 2008.","options":["in 106 CE","in 2008","in the first century BCE","in 2020"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U1-LS2-A2","type":"mcq","stage":"guided","instructions":"Guided practice: Claims and evidence. Choose.","instructions_ar":"الادعاءات والأدلة: اختر.","items":[{"q":"Which evidence BEST supports the claim that the Nabataeans grew rich from trade?","answer":"Hegra stood on the incense route, so caravans stopped there to pay taxes.","explanation":"Evidence.","explanation_ar":"الموقع على طريق البخور.","options":["Giant sandstone rocks rise out of the sand.","The tombs are many times taller than a person.","Visitors come to AlUla today.","Hegra stood on the incense route, so caravans stopped there to pay taxes."],"dok":2,"skill":"claims and evidence","stage":"guided"},{"q":"How do scientists know exactly when many Hegra tombs were carved?","answer":"The inscriptions give the year each tomb was made.","explanation":"Written evidence.","explanation_ar":"النقوش تذكر السنة.","options":["The inscriptions give the year each tomb was made.","They guessed from the color of the rock.","Roman books list every tomb.","Visitors told them."],"dok":2,"skill":"claims and evidence","stage":"guided"},{"q":"What does the unfinished lower part of Qasr al-Farid tell archaeologists?","answer":"Workers carved the tombs from the top down.","explanation":"Inference from evidence.","explanation_ar":"النحت من الأعلى إلى الأسفل.","options":["The owner did not like the tomb.","The rock was too soft.","The tomb was built by the Romans.","Workers carved the tombs from the top down."],"dok":2,"skill":"claims and evidence","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which kind of evidence are the wells cut into the rock at Hegra?","answer":"buildings and structures","explanation":"Kinds of evidence.","explanation_ar":"مبانٍ ومنشآت.","options":["written evidence","buildings and structures","artifacts like pots","location only"],"dok":2,"skill":"kinds of evidence","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Why did Hegra decline?","answer":"More goods traveled by ship across the Red Sea, so fewer caravans crossed the desert.","explanation":"Cause.","explanation_ar":"تحوّل التجارة إلى البحر.","options":["An earthquake destroyed the tombs.","The Nabataeans moved to Riyadh.","More goods traveled by ship across the Red Sea, so fewer caravans crossed the desert.","The wells were full of sand."],"dok":2,"skill":"cause and effect","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which sentence about the Nabataeans does the article NOT give evidence for?","answer":"The Nabataeans loved music and poetry.","explanation":"No evidence.","explanation_ar":"لا دليل في المقال.","options":["The Nabataeans were experts at finding water.","The Nabataeans loved music and poetry.","The Nabataean script developed into Arabic.","The Nabataeans grew rich from the incense trade."],"dok":3,"skill":"evaluating claims","stage":"guided"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U1-LS2-A3","type":"highlight","stage":"freer","instructions":"Find the evidence: Find the evidence.","instructions_ar":"ابحث عن الدليل.","items":[{"q":"Underline the sentence that explains why a city grew in the middle of the desert.","answer":"The answer is trade.","explanation":"Claim.","explanation_ar":"الادعاء.","evidence":"The answer is trade.","text_id":"TXT-RW-L06-U1-LS2-1","dok":2,"skill":"claims and evidence","stage":"freer","sentences":["In the desert near AlUla, in northwest Saudi Arabia, giant sandstone rocks rise out of the sand like ships on a golden sea.","Carved into these rocks are more than one hundred great tombs, some of them many times taller than a person.","This is Hegra, also known as Mada'in Salih – an ancient city built by a people called the Nabataeans about two thousand years ago.","**Who Were the Nabataeans?**","The Nabataeans were Arab traders who built a powerful kingdom across parts of what are today Jordan, southern Syria and northwest Saudi Arabia.","Their capital was Petra, in Jordan, and Hegra was their most important city in the south.","From about the first century BCE to the first century CE, Hegra was a busy stop for caravans.","**Rich from Incense**","Why did a city grow in the middle of the desert?","The answer is trade.","Camel caravans carried frankincense and myrrh – sweet-smelling tree resins from southern Arabia – north to Egypt, Syria and the lands around the Mediterranean Sea.","People used them in medicine and perfume, and they were worth a fortune.","Hegra stood on this incense route, so caravans stopped there to rest, buy water and pay taxes.","The Nabataeans grew rich by protecting and serving these travelers.","**Masters of Water**","Living in the desert meant finding water, and the Nabataeans were experts at it.","At Hegra, they cut dozens of wells deep into the rock to reach water under the ground, and they carefully collected the rare rain.","This skill allowed people and camels to live where few others could survive.","**What the Tombs Tell Us**","The Nabataeans did not leave many books, but they left something just as useful: their tombs.","Archaeologists have counted 111 monumental tombs at Hegra.","Above many of the doors is an inscription in the Nabataean script.","These inscriptions name the owner of the tomb, give the year it was made and even list the fines people would pay for using it without permission.","Because of these dates, scientists know exactly when many tombs were carved.","Interestingly, the Nabataean script later developed into the Arabic alphabet.","The most famous tomb is Qasr al-Farid, which means \"the Lonely Castle,\" because it stands alone, carved from a single huge rock.","Its upper part is finished, but its lower part was never completed.","This tells archaeologists that workers carved the tombs from the top down.","**Decline and Discovery**","In 106 CE, the Roman Empire took control of the Nabataean kingdom.","Over the following centuries, more goods traveled by ship across the Red Sea, and fewer caravans crossed the desert.","Slowly, Hegra declined and was left to the sand and the wind.","However, the dry desert air preserved its tombs remarkably well.","In 2008, Hegra became Saudi Arabia's first UNESCO World Heritage Site.","Today, visitors from around the world come to AlUla to walk among the tombs and imagine the caravans that once filled the city with the smell of incense."]},{"q":"Underline the sentence that explains why the tombs are still in good condition.","answer":"However, the dry desert air preserved its tombs remarkably well.","explanation":"Evidence.","explanation_ar":"الدليل.","evidence":"However, the dry desert air preserved its tombs remarkably well.","text_id":"TXT-RW-L06-U1-LS2-1","dok":2,"skill":"cause and effect","stage":"freer","sentences":["In the desert near AlUla, in northwest Saudi Arabia, giant sandstone rocks rise out of the sand like ships on a golden sea.","Carved into these rocks are more than one hundred great tombs, some of them many times taller than a person.","This is Hegra, also known as Mada'in Salih – an ancient city built by a people called the Nabataeans about two thousand years ago.","**Who Were the Nabataeans?**","The Nabataeans were Arab traders who built a powerful kingdom across parts of what are today Jordan, southern Syria and northwest Saudi Arabia.","Their capital was Petra, in Jordan, and Hegra was their most important city in the south.","From about the first century BCE to the first century CE, Hegra was a busy stop for caravans.","**Rich from Incense**","Why did a city grow in the middle of the desert?","The answer is trade.","Camel caravans carried frankincense and myrrh – sweet-smelling tree resins from southern Arabia – north to Egypt, Syria and the lands around the Mediterranean Sea.","People used them in medicine and perfume, and they were worth a fortune.","Hegra stood on this incense route, so caravans stopped there to rest, buy water and pay taxes.","The Nabataeans grew rich by protecting and serving these travelers.","**Masters of Water**","Living in the desert meant finding water, and the Nabataeans were experts at it.","At Hegra, they cut dozens of wells deep into the rock to reach water under the ground, and they carefully collected the rare rain.","This skill allowed people and camels to live where few others could survive.","**What the Tombs Tell Us**","The Nabataeans did not leave many books, but they left something just as useful: their tombs.","Archaeologists have counted 111 monumental tombs at Hegra.","Above many of the doors is an inscription in the Nabataean script.","These inscriptions name the owner of the tomb, give the year it was made and even list the fines people would pay for using it without permission.","Because of these dates, scientists know exactly when many tombs were carved.","Interestingly, the Nabataean script later developed into the Arabic alphabet.","The most famous tomb is Qasr al-Farid, which means \"the Lonely Castle,\" because it stands alone, carved from a single huge rock.","Its upper part is finished, but its lower part was never completed.","This tells archaeologists that workers carved the tombs from the top down.","**Decline and Discovery**","In 106 CE, the Roman Empire took control of the Nabataean kingdom.","Over the following centuries, more goods traveled by ship across the Red Sea, and fewer caravans crossed the desert.","Slowly, Hegra declined and was left to the sand and the wind.","However, the dry desert air preserved its tombs remarkably well.","In 2008, Hegra became Saudi Arabia's first UNESCO World Heritage Site.","Today, visitors from around the world come to AlUla to walk among the tombs and imagine the caravans that once filled the city with the smell of incense."]},{"q":"Why does the author begin with *like ships on a golden sea*?","answer":"to help readers picture the huge rocks rising from the sand","explanation":"Author's craft.","explanation_ar":"ليتخيل القارئ الصخور.","options":["to help readers picture the huge rocks rising from the sand","to say that Hegra was a port","to describe the Red Sea","to explain how ships carried incense"],"dok":2,"skill":"author's craft","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Why does the author say the tombs were *just as useful* as books?","answer":"Their inscriptions and designs give information about the people, just as books do.","explanation":"Inference.","explanation_ar":"لأن نقوشها تقدم معلومات.","options":["People read stories inside the tombs.","The tombs were used as libraries.","Their inscriptions and designs give information about the people, just as books do.","Books were carved on the tomb walls."],"dok":3,"skill":"inference","stage":"freer"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U1-LS2-A4","type":"open","stage":"production","instructions":"Writing: Explain claims and evidence.","instructions_ar":"اشرح الادعاءات والأدلة.","items":[{"q":"Explain how Hegra became rich. Use evidence from the article.","answer":"Hegra became rich from the incense trade. It stood on the incense route, so caravans stopped there to rest, buy water and pay taxes.","explanation":"Claim + evidence.","explanation_ar":"ادعاء ودليل.","dok":2,"skill":"claims and evidence","stage":"production"},{"q":"Name three kinds of evidence from Hegra and what each one tells us.","answer":"Inscriptions tell us owners' names and dates. Wells show that the Nabataeans were experts with water. The unfinished tomb shows that workers carved from the top down.","explanation":"Kinds of evidence.","explanation_ar":"أنواع الأدلة.","dok":2,"skill":"kinds of evidence","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write the decline of Hegra as a cause–effect chain with arrows.","answer":"Romans took control → more goods traveled by ship → fewer caravans crossed the desert → Hegra declined.","explanation":"Chain.","explanation_ar":"سلسلة.","dok":3,"skill":"cause and effect","stage":"production"},{"q":"If you were an archaeologist at Hegra, what new evidence would you look for and why?","answer":"I would look for coins, because coins from other lands would show where the traders came from.","explanation":"Apply.","explanation_ar":"طبّق.","dok":3,"skill":"apply","stage":"production"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U1-LS2-TR-1","type":"timed_reading","instructions":"Timed reading: Read \"Hegra: The City Carved in Stone\" aloud or silently. Try to finish in the time.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ النص وحاول أن تنهيه في الوقت المحدد.","items":[{"q":"Read “Hegra: The City Carved in Stone”.","answer":"493 words","title":"Hegra: The City Carved in Stone","body":"In the desert near AlUla, in northwest Saudi Arabia, giant sandstone rocks rise out of the sand like ships on a golden sea. Carved into these rocks are more than one hundred great tombs, some of them many times taller than a person. This is Hegra, also known as Mada'in Salih – an ancient city built by a people called the Nabataeans about two thousand years ago.\n\n**Who Were the Nabataeans?**\n\nThe Nabataeans were Arab traders who built a powerful kingdom across parts of what are today Jordan, southern Syria and northwest Saudi Arabia. Their capital was Petra, in Jordan, and Hegra was their most important city in the south. From about the first century BCE to the first century CE, Hegra was a busy stop for caravans.\n\n**Rich from Incense**\n\nWhy did a city grow in the middle of the desert? The answer is trade. Camel caravans carried frankincense and myrrh – sweet-smelling tree resins from southern Arabia – north to Egypt, Syria and the lands around the Mediterranean Sea. People used them in medicine and perfume, and they were worth a fortune. Hegra stood on this incense route, so caravans stopped there to rest, buy water and pay taxes. The Nabataeans grew rich by protecting and serving these travelers.\n\n**Masters of Water**\n\nLiving in the desert meant finding water, and the Nabataeans were experts at it. At Hegra, they cut dozens of wells deep into the rock to reach water under the ground, and they carefully collected the rare rain. This skill allowed people and camels to live where few others could survive.\n\n**What the Tombs Tell Us**\n\nThe Nabataeans did not leave many books, but they left something just as useful: their tombs. Archaeologists have counted 111 monumental tombs at Hegra. Above many of the doors is an inscription in the Nabataean script. These inscriptions name the owner of the tomb, give the year it was made and even list the fines people would pay for using it without permission. Because of these dates, scientists know exactly when many tombs were carved. Interestingly, the Nabataean script later developed into the Arabic alphabet.\n\nThe most famous tomb is Qasr al-Farid, which means \"the Lonely Castle,\" because it stands alone, carved from a single huge rock. Its upper part is finished, but its lower part was never completed. This tells archaeologists that workers carved the tombs from the top down.\n\n**Decline and Discovery**\n\nIn 106 CE, the Roman Empire took control of the Nabataean kingdom. Over the following centuries, more goods traveled by ship across the Red Sea, and fewer caravans crossed the desert. Slowly, Hegra declined and was left to the sand and the wind. However, the dry desert air preserved its tombs remarkably well.\n\nIn 2008, Hegra became Saudi Arabia's first UNESCO World Heritage Site. Today, visitors from around the world come to AlUla to walk among the tombs and imagine the caravans that once filled the city with the smell of incense.","words":493,"limit":272}],"time_limit_sec":272},{"id":"RW-L06-U1-LS2-ORG","type":"organizer","organizer":"Claim–Evidence Chart","instructions":"Organizer: Write each claim and the evidence from the article that proves it.","items":[{"q":"Claim 1: Hegra grew rich.","answer":"Hegra grew rich from the incense trade."},{"q":"Evidence for Claim 1","answer":"Hegra stood on the incense route, so caravans stopped there to rest, buy water and pay taxes."},{"q":"Claim 2: The Nabataeans were experts with water.","answer":"The Nabataeans were experts at finding and storing water."},{"q":"Evidence for Claim 2","answer":"They cut dozens of wells deep into the rock and collected the rare rain."},{"q":"Claim 3: Workers carved the tombs from the top down.","answer":"The tombs were carved from the top down."},{"q":"Evidence for Claim 3","answer":"The upper part of Qasr al-Farid is finished, but the lower part was never completed."}]}],"game":{"name":"Evidence Hunters","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["On this screen: answer the lesson questions as fast as you can.","In class: Dig and match: the child 'excavates' evidence cards from the sand and drags each one to the claim it proves. Correct matches move a camel caravan one stop along the incense route to Hegra.","Teams draw a claim card.","They search the evidence cards for the proof and read it aloud.","They explain the link in one sentence: 'This shows that…'.","A wrong match sends the caravan back one stop.","Correct match = 2 points; clear explanation = 1 point."],"data":[]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"What is *evidence* in a history text?","answer":"facts, objects or writing that show a claim is true","explanation":"Definition.","explanation_ar":"الدليل.","options":["the title of the text","the writer's favorite idea","a picture with no information","facts, objects or writing that show a claim is true"],"dok":1,"skill":"claims and evidence"},{"q":"What is a *tomb*?","answer":"a large grave or a stone room where a dead person is buried","explanation":"Vocabulary.","explanation_ar":"مقبرة.","options":["a large grave or a stone room where a dead person is buried","a kind of well","a market","a camel saddle"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary"},{"q":"*Workers **carved** the tombs out of solid rock.* *Carved* means ___.","answer":"cut a hard material to make a shape","explanation":"Carve.","explanation_ar":"نحتوا.","options":["painted with colors","built with bricks","covered with sand","cut a hard material to make a shape"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary in context"},{"q":"Which people built Hegra?","answer":"the Nabataeans","explanation":"Key detail.","explanation_ar":"الأنباط.","options":["the Romans","the Nabataeans","the Egyptians","the Greeks"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details"},{"q":"Which sentence about the Nabataeans is correct?","answer":"The Nabataeans were skilled traders.","explanation":"Plural → were.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["The Nabataeans was skilled traders.","The Nabataeans were skilled traders.","The Nabataeans is skilled traders.","The Nabataeans being skilled traders."],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar"},{"q":"Which kind of evidence is a Nabataean inscription?","answer":"written evidence","explanation":"Kinds of evidence.","explanation_ar":"دليل مكتوب.","options":["written evidence","an artifact like a pot","a well","a trade route"],"dok":2,"skill":"kinds of evidence"},{"q":"Why did caravans stop at Hegra?","answer":"to rest, buy water and pay taxes","explanation":"Key detail.","explanation_ar":"للراحة وشراء الماء ودفع الضرائب.","options":["to build ships","to carve their own tombs","to rest, buy water and pay taxes","to sell cars"],"dok":2,"skill":"key details"},{"q":"What was the effect of more goods traveling by ship across the Red Sea?","answer":"Fewer caravans crossed the desert, and Hegra declined.","explanation":"Cause → effect.","explanation_ar":"تراجعت الحِجر.","options":["Fewer caravans crossed the desert, and Hegra declined.","Hegra built a port.","The tombs were finished faster.","More caravans came to Hegra."],"dok":2,"skill":"cause and effect"},{"q":"Why is the Nabataean script important for Arabic speakers today?","answer":"It later developed into the Arabic alphabet.","explanation":"Inference.","explanation_ar":"تطوّر إلى الأبجدية العربية.","options":["It is the same as English.","It was used only on coins.","It later developed into the Arabic alphabet.","It has no letters."],"dok":3,"skill":"inference"},{"q":"A friend says, *The Nabataeans were bad at building.* Which evidence from the article shows this claim is WRONG?","answer":"They carved 111 monumental tombs and cut dozens of wells into rock.","explanation":"Evaluate a claim.","explanation_ar":"نحتوا 111 مقبرة وحفروا آبارًا.","options":["Hegra declined after 106 CE.","The Romans took control.","They carved 111 monumental tombs and cut dozens of wells into rock.","Visitors come to AlUla."],"dok":3,"skill":"evaluating claims"}],"images":[{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS2-V01","prompt":"Picture for the word 'tomb': a carved doorway in a rock face"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS2-V02","prompt":"Picture for the word 'carve': a worker with a chisel cutting stone"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS2-V03","prompt":"Picture for the word 'incense': smoke rising from a small burner"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS2-V04","prompt":"Picture for the word 'resin': drops of hardened tree resin"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS2-V05","prompt":"Picture for the word 'tax': a trader handing coins to an official"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS2-V06","prompt":"Picture for the word 'fine': a hand paying coins with a warning sign"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS2-V07","prompt":"Picture for the word 'script': ancient letters next to Arabic letters"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS2-01","prompt":"Carved tombs of Hegra and the Lonely Castle."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS2-02","prompt":"Map of the ancient incense route through Hegra."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS2-03","prompt":"A claim card linked to an evidence card."}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS2-V01","script":"tomb. The tomb was carved into a sandstone rock."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS2-V02","script":"carve. Workers carved the tombs by hand."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS2-V03","script":"incense. Caravans carried incense across Arabia."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS2-V04","script":"resin. Frankincense is a resin from a tree."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS2-V05","script":"tax. Caravans paid taxes in Hegra."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS2-V06","script":"fine. The inscription lists a fine for misusing the tomb."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS2-V07","script":"script. The Nabataean script developed into Arabic."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS2-T1","script":"In the desert near AlUla, in northwest Saudi Arabia, giant sandstone rocks rise out of the sand like ships on a golden sea. Carved into these rocks are more than one hundred great tombs, some of them many times taller than a person. This is Hegra, also known as Mada'in Salih – an ancient city built by a people called the Nabataeans about two thousand years ago. **Who Were the Nabataeans?** The Nabataeans were Arab traders who built a powerful kingdom across parts of what are today Jordan, southern Syria and northwest Saudi Arabia. Their capital was Petra, in Jordan, and Hegra was their most important city in the south. From about the first century BCE to the first century CE, Hegra was a busy stop for caravans. **Rich from Incense** Why did a city grow in the middle of the desert? The answer is trade. Camel caravans carried frankincense and myrrh – sweet-smelling tree resins from southern Arabia – north to Egypt, Syria and the lands around the Mediterranean Sea. People used them in medicine and perfume, and they were worth a fortune. Hegra stood on this incense route, so caravans stopped there to rest, buy water and pay taxes. The Nabataeans grew rich by protecting and serving these travelers. **Masters of Water** Living in the desert meant finding water, and the Nabataeans were experts at it. At Hegra, they cut dozens of wells deep into the rock to reach water under the ground, and they carefully collected the rare rain. This skill allowed people and camels to live where few others could survive. **What the Tombs Tell Us** The Nabataeans did not leave many books, but they left something just as useful: their tombs. Archaeologists have counted 111 monumental tombs at Hegra. Above many of the doors is an inscription in the Nabataean script. These inscriptions name the owner of the tomb, give the year it was made and even list the fines people would pay for using it without permission. Because of these dates, scientists know exactly when many tombs were carved. Interestingly, the Nabataean script later developed into the Arabic alphabet. The most famous tomb is Qasr al-Farid, which means \"the Lonely Castle,\" because it stands alone, carved from a single huge rock. Its upper part is finished, but its lower part was never completed. This tells archaeologists that workers carved the tombs from the top down. **Decline and Discovery** In 106 CE, the Roman Empire took control of the Nabataean kingdom. Over the following centuries, more goods traveled by ship across the Red Sea, and fewer caravans crossed the desert. Slowly, Hegra declined and was left to the sand and the wind. However, the dry desert air preserved its tombs remarkably well. In 2008, Hegra became Saudi Arabia's first UNESCO World Heritage Site. Today, visitors from around the world come to AlUla to walk among the tombs and imagine the caravans that once filled the city with the smell of incense."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS2-VID","script":"Giant rocks rise from the sand like ships on a golden sea… Welcome to Hegra! Camel caravans carried frankincense and myrrh north. Hegra stood on the incense route… Wells cut deep into rock… inscriptions above the doors… a tomb left unfinished at the bottom. Claim: workers carved from the top down. Evidence: the lower part was never finished! Ships replaced caravans, and Hegra declined – but the dry air kept its secrets safe."}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U1-LS3","title":"Chronological text structure: \"The Caravan Boy of Dedan\"","lesson_type":"strategy_paired_text","objectives":["identify chronological (time-order) structure in a story and in a history article","use time signals (before dawn, by the third day, as soon as, centuries later) and dates (BCE / CE) to put events in order","compare how the story and the article use time: days and years vs. centuries"],"objectives_ar":["تحديد البنية الزمنية (الترتيب الزمني) في قصة وفي مقال تاريخي.","استخدام مؤشرات الزمن والتواريخ لترتيب الأحداث.","مقارنة طريقة استخدام القصة والمقال للزمن: أيام وسنوات مقابل قرون."],"success_criteria":["I can find the time signals in the story and use them to retell it in order.","I can build a timeline of Hegra's history with dates from the article.","I can explain how the two texts are organized by time in different ways."],"success_criteria_ar":["أستطيع إيجاد مؤشرات الزمن في القصة واستخدامها لإعادة سردها بالترتيب.","أستطيع بناء خط زمني لتاريخ الحِجر بالتواريخ من المقال.","أستطيع شرح كيف ينتظم النصان زمنيًا بطريقتين مختلفتين."],"map_area":"Literary Text: Language, Craft, and Structure","vocabulary":[{"word":"guide","pos":"noun / verb","ipa":"/ɡaɪd/","definition":"a person who shows others the way; to show the way","arabic":"دليل / مرشد / يرشد","example":"The caravan guide knew the desert well.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS3-V01","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS3-V01","tier":"core"},{"word":"fiercely","pos":"adverb","ipa":"/ˈfɪrsli/","definition":"in a very strong, powerful way","arabic":"بشدة / بضراوة","example":"The sun burned fiercely at noon.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS3-V02","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS3-V02","tier":"core"},{"word":"howl","pos":"verb","ipa":"/haʊl/","definition":"to make a long, loud sound, like a strong wind or a wolf","arabic":"يعوي / يزمجر","example":"The wind howled through the rocks.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS3-V03","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS3-V03","tier":"core"},{"word":"kneel","pos":"verb","ipa":"/nil/","definition":"to go down onto your knees (past: knelt)","arabic":"يبرك / يركع","example":"The camels knelt in a circle.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS3-V04","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS3-V04","tier":"core"},{"word":"shelter","pos":"verb / noun","ipa":"/ˈʃɛltər/","definition":"to stay in a safe place away from danger; a safe place","arabic":"يحتمي / مأوى","example":"They sheltered behind the camels.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS3-V05","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS3-V05","tier":"core"},{"word":"crowd","pos":"verb / noun","ipa":"/kraʊd/","definition":"to come together in a large group; a large group of people","arabic":"يتزاحم / حشد","example":"Traders crowded around the wells.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS3-V06","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS3-V06","tier":"core"},{"word":"timeline","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈtaɪmˌlaɪn/","definition":"a line that shows events in the order they happened","arabic":"خط زمني","example":"We drew a timeline of Hegra's history.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS3-V07","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS3-V07","tier":"core"}],"explanation":{"text":"A text with **chronological structure** tells events in **time order** – from first to last. Stories, biographies and history texts often use it. To follow it, look for **time signals**: *Before dawn, On the first day, That night, By the third day, When…, As soon as…, Five days later, Many years later*.\n\nHistory texts also use **dates**. **BCE** (Before the Common Era) counts **down** to year 1; **CE** (the Common Era) counts **up** from year 1. So *the first century BCE* comes **before** *the first century CE*, and *106 CE* comes before *2008*. A **timeline** helps you see the order and the **gaps** between events.\n\nTexts can use time in different ways: a **story** may cover a few **days** in detail and then jump *many years later*; a **history article** may cover **centuries** quickly. Ask: *How much time does the text cover? Where does it slow down or jump ahead? Why?*","examples":["Story signal: ***Before dawn,*** Hani tied the last bag onto his camel. → the story begins.","Story signal: ***On the first day,*** the sun burned so fiercely… → day 1.","Story signal: ***That night,*** old Saad pointed at the sky. → night 1.","Story signal: ***By the third day,*** the wind began to howl. → day 3.","Story signal: ***As soon as*** the first stars appeared, he found Banat Na'sh. → one event right after another.","Story signal: ***Five days later*** … ***Many years later*** → the story jumps ahead.","Article dates: *first century BCE → first century CE → 106 CE → 2008*.","Article signal: ***Over the following centuries,*** more goods traveled by ship. → a long period told quickly.","Comparison: the story covers about **one week** plus a jump; the article covers about **two thousand years**."],"common_errors":["✗ Thinking 100 BCE comes after 100 CE. → ✓ 100 BCE comes before 100 CE. (BCE counts down to year 1.)","✗ Retelling events in the order you remember them. → ✓ Use the time signals to put events in order. (Follow the signals.)","✗ Missing a time jump (*Many years later*). → ✓ Notice when the story jumps ahead. (Look for jumps.)","✗ The camels kneel in a circle yesterday. → ✓ The camels knelt in a circle yesterday. (Past of kneel → **knelt**.)","✗ As soon as the stars appeared, he finds the stars. → ✓ As soon as the stars appeared, he found the stars. (Keep the past tense.)","✗ Writing dates without BCE / CE. → ✓ Write *the first century BCE*. (Add BCE or CE.)","✗ A timeline with no order. → ✓ Put the oldest event on the left. (Left = oldest.)"],"arabic":"البنية الزمنية تعرض الأحداث بترتيب حدوثها من الأول إلى الأخير، وتُستخدم في القصص والسير الذاتية ونصوص التاريخ. ابحث عن مؤشرات الزمن مثل Before dawn وBy the third day وAs soon as وMany years later.\n\nتستخدم نصوص التاريخ التواريخ: BCE تعني قبل الميلاد وتعدّ تنازليًا حتى السنة الأولى، وCE تعني بعد الميلاد وتعدّ تصاعديًا. والخط الزمني يساعدك على رؤية الترتيب والفجوات بين الأحداث.\n\nتختلف النصوص في استخدام الزمن: القصة قد تغطي أيامًا قليلة بالتفصيل ثم تقفز سنوات، والمقال التاريخي قد يغطي قرونًا بسرعة.\n\nانتبه: ماضي kneel هو knelt، وحافظ على زمن الماضي في السرد."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:30","narration":"Before dawn, a twelve-year-old boy ties the last bag of frankincense onto his camel…","on_screen":"The Caravan Boy of Dedan 🐪","visual":"Palm gardens of ancient Dedan in blue morning light; a caravan prepares to leave."},{"time":"00:30–01:45","narration":"On the first day… That night… By the third day, the wind began to howl…","on_screen":"Day 1 · Night 1 · Day 3","visual":"A burning sun; stars over the dunes; a brown wall of sand rolls toward the caravan."},{"time":"01:45–02:40","narration":"As soon as the first stars appeared, he found Banat Na'sh. Five days later, the caravan reached the wells of Tayma.","on_screen":"As soon as · Five days later · Many years later","visual":"Hani points to seven stars; a busy market around the wells of Tayma."},{"time":"02:40–03:30","narration":"Time signals and dates build a text's backbone – like stops along a caravan route.","on_screen":"Time signals + dates = chronological structure","visual":"A timeline appears as a caravan route with stops for each event."},{"time":"03:30–04:00","narration":"One week in the story, two thousand years in the article – both in time order!","on_screen":"Story: days · Article: centuries","visual":"Two timelines, one short and detailed, one long and fast."}],"captions_ar":["قبل الفجر، يربط فتى في الثانية عشرة آخر كيس من اللبان على جمله…","في اليوم الأول… وفي تلك الليلة… وبحلول اليوم الثالث بدأت الريح تزمجر…","ما إن ظهرت النجوم الأولى حتى وجد بنات نعش. وبعد خمسة أيام وصلت القافلة إلى آبار تيماء.","مؤشرات الزمن والتواريخ هي العمود الفقري للنص، كمحطات على طريق القافلة.","أسبوع واحد في القصة، وألفا عام في المقال، وكلاهما بترتيب زمني!"],"voice":"Narrator: American English, warm storyteller male voice, speed 1.0. Separate voices for Hani, his father and Saad."},"texts":[{"type":"reading","title":"The Caravan Boy of Dedan","body":"Before dawn, Hani tied the last bag of frankincense onto his camel and looked back at the palm gardens of Dedan. He was twelve, and this was his first journey with his father's caravan.\n\n\"Stay close, watch the camels and never waste water,\" his father said.\n\nOn the first day, the sun burned so fiercely that Hani's lips cracked. He wanted to drink the whole water skin, but he remembered his father's words and took only small sips.\n\nThat night, old Saad, the caravan guide, pointed at the sky. \"The desert has no roads,\" he said, \"but the stars never lie. Those seven stars, Banat Na'sh, always turn around the north. Find them, and you will find your way.\"\n\nBy the third day, the wind began to howl. A wall of sand rolled toward them like a brown wave. The men made the camels kneel in a circle, and everyone sheltered behind them. Hani pressed his scarf over his face and counted his breaths until the storm passed.\n\nWhen the air finally cleared, the path had disappeared under the sand. The men argued about which way to go. Hani remembered Saad's lesson. As soon as the first stars appeared, he found Banat Na'sh and pointed. \"North is there,\" he said. Saad smiled and nodded.\n\nFive days later, the caravan reached the wells of Tayma. Traders crowded around them, buying incense with silver and grain. Hani's father placed a hand on his shoulder. \"You did not only follow the caravan,\" he said. \"Tonight, you helped to lead it.\"\n\nMany years later, when Hani became a caravan leader himself, he always told new boys the same thing: \"Watch the camels, save your water and trust the stars.\"","word_count":285,"audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS3-T1","id":"TXT-RW-L06-U1-LS3-1","genre":"narrative","role":"paired","difficulty":"700L–850L","glossary":[{"word":"Dedan","definition":"an ancient kingdom and oasis city near today's AlUla","arabic":"دادان (ديدان)"},{"word":"water skin","definition":"a bag made of animal skin for carrying water","arabic":"قِربة"},{"word":"Banat Na'sh","definition":"the Arabic name for the seven bright stars that people in English call the Big Dipper","arabic":"بنات نعش"}]}],"activities":[{"id":"RW-L06-U1-LS3-A1","type":"mcq","stage":"controlled","instructions":"Controlled practice: Read *The Caravan Boy of Dedan*. Choose the answer.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ القصة واختر الإجابة.","items":[{"q":"What did Hani load onto his camel before dawn?","answer":"the last bag of frankincense","explanation":"Paragraph 1.","explanation_ar":"آخر كيس من اللبان.","options":["a box of dates","his father's sword","the last bag of frankincense","a tent"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"How old was Hani on his first caravan journey?","answer":"twelve","explanation":"Paragraph 1.","explanation_ar":"اثنا عشر.","options":["ten","twelve","fifteen","eight"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What did Hani do on the first day when he wanted to drink all his water?","answer":"He took only small sips.","explanation":"Paragraph 3.","explanation_ar":"شرب رشفات صغيرة.","options":["He drank the whole water skin.","He gave his water to the camels.","He went back to Dedan.","He took only small sips."],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What did Saad teach Hani on the first night?","answer":"to find the north by the seven stars of Banat Na'sh","explanation":"Paragraph 4.","explanation_ar":"معرفة الشمال ببنات نعش.","options":["to ride a camel","to buy incense","to build a tent","to find the north by the seven stars of Banat Na'sh"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"How did the caravan shelter from the sandstorm?","answer":"The camels knelt in a circle, and everyone sheltered behind them.","explanation":"Paragraph 5.","explanation_ar":"برّكوا الجمال في دائرة واحتموا خلفها.","options":["They hid in a cave.","The camels knelt in a circle, and everyone sheltered behind them.","They rode faster.","They went back home."],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Where did the caravan arrive five days later?","answer":"at the wells of Tayma","explanation":"Paragraph 7.","explanation_ar":"آبار تيماء.","options":["at the wells of Tayma","at Hegra","at the Red Sea","back in Dedan"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U1-LS3-A2","type":"mcq","stage":"guided","instructions":"Guided practice: Chronological structure. Choose.","instructions_ar":"البنية الزمنية: اختر.","items":[{"q":"Which time signal begins *The Caravan Boy of Dedan*?","answer":"Before dawn","explanation":"Time signal.","explanation_ar":"قبل الفجر.","options":["Five days later","Before dawn","Many years later","By the third day"],"dok":1,"skill":"chronological structure","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which event in Hani's journey happened RIGHT AFTER the sandstorm?","answer":"The men argued because the path had disappeared.","explanation":"Order.","explanation_ar":"اختلف الرجال لأن الطريق اختفى.","options":["Hani loaded the frankincense.","Saad taught Hani about the stars.","The caravan reached Tayma.","The men argued because the path had disappeared."],"dok":2,"skill":"sequence","stage":"guided"},{"q":"What does *As soon as the first stars appeared* tell you about Hani's action?","answer":"He found Banat Na'sh immediately after the stars came out.","explanation":"Time signal.","explanation_ar":"فور ظهور النجوم.","options":["He waited many days.","He found the stars before the storm.","He found Banat Na'sh immediately after the stars came out.","He slept all night."],"dok":2,"skill":"time signals","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Where does the caravan-boy story JUMP far ahead in time?","answer":"Many years later, when Hani became a caravan leader himself","explanation":"Time jump.","explanation_ar":"قفزة زمنية.","options":["Many years later, when Hani became a caravan leader himself","That night, old Saad pointed at the sky","On the first day, the sun burned","By the third day, the wind began to howl"],"dok":2,"skill":"time signals","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which order of dates from the Hegra article is correct (oldest → newest)?","answer":"first century BCE → 106 CE → 2008","explanation":"BCE before CE.","explanation_ar":"الترتيب الصحيح.","options":["2008 → 106 CE → first century BCE","106 CE → first century BCE → 2008","first century BCE → 106 CE → 2008","first century BCE → 2008 → 106 CE"],"dok":2,"skill":"dates","stage":"guided"},{"q":"How is time DIFFERENT in the story and in the Hegra article?","answer":"The story covers about a week in detail; the article covers about two thousand years quickly.","explanation":"Compare.","explanation_ar":"القصة أسبوع بالتفصيل والمقال ألفا عام.","options":["Both cover exactly one day.","The article covers one week; the story covers centuries.","Neither text is in time order.","The story covers about a week in detail; the article covers about two thousand years quickly."],"dok":3,"skill":"comparing texts","stage":"guided"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U1-LS3-A3","type":"organizer","stage":"freer","instructions":"Organizer: Complete the Double Timeline.","instructions_ar":"أكمل الخط الزمني المزدوج.","items":[{"q":"Story: list four events in order, each with its time signal.","answer":"Before dawn – Hani leaves Dedan. That night – Saad teaches him the stars. By the third day – a sandstorm comes. Five days later – they reach Tayma.","explanation":"Story timeline.","explanation_ar":"خط القصة.","dok":2,"skill":"chronological structure","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Article: write Hegra's timeline with four dates or time phrases.","answer":"1st century BCE–1st century CE: busy caravan city → 106 CE: Romans take control → following centuries: Hegra declines → 2008: UNESCO site.","explanation":"Article timeline.","explanation_ar":"خط المقال.","dok":2,"skill":"dates","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Find two time signals in the story that show one event happening right after another.","answer":"When the air finally cleared…; As soon as the first stars appeared…","explanation":"Signals.","explanation_ar":"مؤشرات.","dok":2,"skill":"time signals","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Explain one way the two timelines are different.","answer":"The story slows down to show a few days in detail, but the article moves quickly across many centuries.","explanation":"Compare.","explanation_ar":"قارن.","dok":3,"skill":"comparing texts","stage":"freer"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U1-LS3-A4","type":"open","stage":"production","instructions":"Writing: Retell and connect.","instructions_ar":"أعد السرد واربط.","items":[{"q":"Retell the story in five sentences, using five different time signals.","answer":"Before dawn, Hani left Dedan with his father's caravan. On the first day, he saved his water. That night, Saad taught him to find the north. By the third day, a sandstorm hid the path, but as soon as the stars appeared, Hani showed the way. Five days later, they reached Tayma.","explanation":"Retell.","explanation_ar":"أعد السرد.","dok":2,"skill":"retelling","stage":"production"},{"q":"How does Hani change from the beginning to the end of the story?","answer":"At the beginning, he is a new boy who only follows; at the end, he helps lead the caravan and later becomes a leader himself.","explanation":"Character change.","explanation_ar":"تغيّر الشخصية.","dok":3,"skill":"character change","stage":"production"},{"q":"What does the story help you understand about the incense trade that the article only mentions?","answer":"It shows how hard and dangerous the journeys were – heat, little water, sandstorms and finding the way by the stars.","explanation":"Connect texts.","explanation_ar":"اربط النصين.","dok":3,"skill":"comparing texts","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write a time signal and a sentence to add a new event between the storm and Tayma.","answer":"Two days later, the caravan found a small well and filled every water skin.","explanation":"Create.","explanation_ar":"أنشئ.","dok":2,"skill":"time signals","stage":"production"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U1-LS3-TR-1","type":"timed_reading","instructions":"Timed reading: Read \"The Caravan Boy of Dedan\" aloud or silently. Try to finish in the time.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ النص وحاول أن تنهيه في الوقت المحدد.","items":[{"q":"Read “The Caravan Boy of Dedan”.","answer":"285 words","title":"The Caravan Boy of Dedan","body":"Before dawn, Hani tied the last bag of frankincense onto his camel and looked back at the palm gardens of Dedan. He was twelve, and this was his first journey with his father's caravan.\n\n\"Stay close, watch the camels and never waste water,\" his father said.\n\nOn the first day, the sun burned so fiercely that Hani's lips cracked. He wanted to drink the whole water skin, but he remembered his father's words and took only small sips.\n\nThat night, old Saad, the caravan guide, pointed at the sky. \"The desert has no roads,\" he said, \"but the stars never lie. Those seven stars, Banat Na'sh, always turn around the north. Find them, and you will find your way.\"\n\nBy the third day, the wind began to howl. A wall of sand rolled toward them like a brown wave. The men made the camels kneel in a circle, and everyone sheltered behind them. Hani pressed his scarf over his face and counted his breaths until the storm passed.\n\nWhen the air finally cleared, the path had disappeared under the sand. The men argued about which way to go. Hani remembered Saad's lesson. As soon as the first stars appeared, he found Banat Na'sh and pointed. \"North is there,\" he said. Saad smiled and nodded.\n\nFive days later, the caravan reached the wells of Tayma. Traders crowded around them, buying incense with silver and grain. Hani's father placed a hand on his shoulder. \"You did not only follow the caravan,\" he said. \"Tonight, you helped to lead it.\"\n\nMany years later, when Hani became a caravan leader himself, he always told new boys the same thing: \"Watch the camels, save your water and trust the stars.\"","words":285,"limit":163}],"time_limit_sec":163},{"id":"RW-L06-U1-LS3-ORG","type":"organizer","organizer":"Double Timeline","instructions":"Organizer: Put the events of each text in time order with their time signals or dates. Then compare.","items":[{"q":"Story – beginning (Before dawn …)","answer":"Before dawn, Hani loads frankincense and leaves Dedan with his father's caravan."},{"q":"Story – the journey (On the first day … By the third day …)","answer":"Day 1: he saves water. Night 1: Saad teaches him Banat Na'sh. Day 3: a sandstorm hides the path."},{"q":"Story – the turning point (As soon as …)","answer":"As soon as the stars appear, Hani finds Banat Na'sh and points north."},{"q":"Story – the ending (Five days later … Many years later …)","answer":"Five days later: they reach Tayma; his father praises him. Many years later: Hani is a leader."},{"q":"Article – Hegra timeline with dates","answer":"1st century BCE–1st century CE: busy caravan city → 106 CE: Romans take control → following centuries: decline → 2008: UNESCO site"},{"q":"How the two timelines are different","answer":"The story covers about a week in detail and then jumps; the article covers about 2,000 years quickly with dates."}]}],"game":{"name":"Caravan Timeline Race","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["On this screen: answer the lesson questions as fast as you can.","In class: Caravan route: the child drags events and dates onto a route from Dedan to Tayma (story) or from BCE to today (history). Correct order moves the camel caravan forward; a wrong order brings a sandstorm that shuffles one card.","Teams get a shuffled set of event cards.","They lay them on the floor timeline in order and add the matching time signal.","The teacher checks; the camel token moves one stop for every correct card.","Bonus round: place the article dates (BCE before CE!).","First team with a full, correct timeline = 3 bonus points."],"data":[]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"What is *chronological structure*?","answer":"telling events in time order, from first to last","explanation":"Definition.","explanation_ar":"الترتيب الزمني.","options":["telling events from most to least important","describing a place from top to bottom","comparing two things","telling events in time order, from first to last"],"dok":1,"skill":"chronological structure"},{"q":"Which of these is a time signal?","answer":"By the third day","explanation":"Signal.","explanation_ar":"مؤشر زمني.","options":["By the third day","In the desert","With his father","Because of the storm"],"dok":1,"skill":"time signals"},{"q":"*The wind began to **howl**.* *Howl* means ___.","answer":"make a long, loud sound","explanation":"Context.","explanation_ar":"تزمجر.","options":["stop completely","become warm","make a long, loud sound","turn green"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary in context"},{"q":"Which comes FIRST in history?","answer":"the first century BCE","explanation":"BCE counts down.","explanation_ar":"القرن الأول قبل الميلاد.","options":["the first century BCE","the first century CE","106 CE","2008"],"dok":1,"skill":"dates"},{"q":"Which sentence about the caravan camels is correct?","answer":"The camels knelt behind the rocks.","explanation":"kneel → knelt.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["The camels kneels behind the rocks last night.","The camels knelt behind the rocks.","The camels kneel behind the rocks last night.","The camels was kneeling behind the rocks."],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar"},{"q":"What happened just BEFORE Hani found Banat Na'sh?","answer":"The first stars appeared after the storm.","explanation":"Sequence.","explanation_ar":"ظهرت النجوم الأولى.","options":["The first stars appeared after the storm.","The caravan reached Tayma.","Hani loaded the frankincense.","Hani became a leader."],"dok":2,"skill":"sequence"},{"q":"Why does a timeline help readers of history?","answer":"It shows the order of events and the time between them.","explanation":"Purpose.","explanation_ar":"يُظهر الترتيب والمدة.","options":["It makes the text shorter.","It shows the writer's opinion.","It lists new vocabulary.","It shows the order of events and the time between them."],"dok":2,"skill":"timelines"},{"q":"Why does Saad say *the stars never lie*?","answer":"The stars are always in the same pattern, so they can show the way.","explanation":"Inference.","explanation_ar":"النجوم ثابتة النمط.","options":["The stars can talk.","Saad cannot read maps.","The stars are always in the same pattern, so they can show the way.","The stars move randomly."],"dok":2,"skill":"inference"},{"q":"Why does the story slow down to describe the sandstorm in detail?","answer":"It is the most exciting and important part, where Hani's skill is tested.","explanation":"Author's choice.","explanation_ar":"لأنها أهم لحظة.","options":["Sandstorms last for years.","It is the most exciting and important part, where Hani's skill is tested.","The writer forgot the other events.","It is the first event."],"dok":3,"skill":"author's craft"},{"q":"Which statement is TRUE about both *The Caravan Boy of Dedan* and the Hegra article?","answer":"Both are organized in time order and mention the incense trade.","explanation":"Compare texts.","explanation_ar":"كلاهما مرتّب زمنيًا ويذكر تجارة البخور.","options":["Both are about Petra.","Both are organized in time order and mention the incense trade.","Both cover only one day.","Both are poems."],"dok":3,"skill":"comparing texts"}],"images":[{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS3-V01","prompt":"Picture for the word 'guide': an old man pointing the way"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS3-V02","prompt":"Picture for the word 'fiercely': a blazing desert sun"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS3-V03","prompt":"Picture for the word 'howl': sand blowing hard past rocks"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS3-V04","prompt":"Picture for the word 'kneel': camels kneeling in the sand"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS3-V05","prompt":"Picture for the word 'shelter': people hiding from a sandstorm behind camels"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS3-V06","prompt":"Picture for the word 'crowd': many traders around a well"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS3-V07","prompt":"Picture for the word 'timeline': a line with dates and pictures"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS3-01","prompt":"Hani prepares his camel before dawn."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS3-02","prompt":"The caravan shelters from a sandstorm."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS3-03","prompt":"A story timeline and a history timeline."}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS3-V01","script":"guide. The caravan guide knew the desert well."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS3-V02","script":"fiercely. The sun burned fiercely at noon."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS3-V03","script":"howl. The wind howled through the rocks."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS3-V04","script":"kneel. The camels knelt in a circle."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS3-V05","script":"shelter. They sheltered behind the camels."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS3-V06","script":"crowd. Traders crowded around the wells."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS3-V07","script":"timeline. We drew a timeline of Hegra's history."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS3-T1","script":"Before dawn, Hani tied the last bag of frankincense onto his camel and looked back at the palm gardens of Dedan. He was twelve, and this was his first journey with his father's caravan. \"Stay close, watch the camels and never waste water,\" his father said. On the first day, the sun burned so fiercely that Hani's lips cracked. He wanted to drink the whole water skin, but he remembered his father's words and took only small sips. That night, old Saad, the caravan guide, pointed at the sky. \"The desert has no roads,\" he said, \"but the stars never lie. Those seven stars, Banat Na'sh, always turn around the north. Find them, and you will find your way.\" By the third day, the wind began to howl. A wall of sand rolled toward them like a brown wave. The men made the camels kneel in a circle, and everyone sheltered behind them. Hani pressed his scarf over his face and counted his breaths until the storm passed. When the air finally cleared, the path had disappeared under the sand. The men argued about which way to go. Hani remembered Saad's lesson. As soon as the first stars appeared, he found Banat Na'sh and pointed. \"North is there,\" he said. Saad smiled and nodded. Five days later, the caravan reached the wells of Tayma. Traders crowded around them, buying incense with silver and grain. Hani's father placed a hand on his shoulder. \"You did not only follow the caravan,\" he said. \"Tonight, you helped to lead it.\" Many years later, when Hani became a caravan leader himself, he always told new boys the same thing: \"Watch the camels, save your water and trust the stars.\""},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS3-VID","script":"Before dawn, a twelve-year-old boy ties the last bag of frankincense onto his camel… On the first day… That night… By the third day, the wind began to howl… As soon as the first stars appeared, he found Banat Na'sh. Five days later, the caravan reached the wells of Tayma. Time signals and dates build a text's backbone – like stops along a caravan route. One week in the story, two thousand years in the article – both in time order!"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U1-LS4","title":"Writing Skill: Paraphrasing facts","lesson_type":"writing_skill","objectives":["take short notes from a factual source with key words, arrows and symbols","paraphrase facts from notes: change everyday words and sentence structure but keep names, numbers, dates and special terms exact","credit the source with *According to…*"],"objectives_ar":["تدوين ملاحظات قصيرة من مصدر معلوماتي بالكلمات المفتاحية والأسهم والرموز.","إعادة صياغة الحقائق من الملاحظات مع تغيير الكلمات العامة وتركيب الجمل والإبقاء على الأسماء والأرقام والتواريخ والمصطلحات كما هي.","نسبة المعلومات إلى مصدرها بعبارة According to."],"success_criteria":["My notes use key words and symbols, not full copied sentences.","My paraphrase keeps every fact correct and exact.","I changed the wording and structure, and I credited the source."],"success_criteria_ar":["ملاحظاتي كلمات مفتاحية ورموز وليست جملًا منسوخة.","إعادة صياغتي تحافظ على صحة كل حقيقة ودقتها.","غيّرت الصياغة والتركيب ونسبت المعلومات إلى المصدر."],"map_area":"Language Usage: Write, Revise Texts for Purpose and Audience (cross-strand)","vocabulary":[{"word":"source","pos":"noun","ipa":"/sɔrs/","definition":"a book, a website or a person that gives you information","arabic":"مصدر","example":"Always check your source.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS4-V01","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS4-V01","tier":"core"},{"word":"notes","pos":"noun","ipa":"/noʊts/","definition":"short words and phrases you write to remember information","arabic":"ملاحظات","example":"Take notes in key words.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS4-V02","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS4-V02","tier":"core"},{"word":"aromatic","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˌærəˈmætɪk/","definition":"having a strong, pleasant smell","arabic":"عطري / ذو رائحة زكية","example":"Frankincense is an aromatic resin.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS4-V03","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS4-V03","tier":"core"},{"word":"harvest","pos":"verb / noun","ipa":"/ˈhɑrvɪst/","definition":"to collect a crop or a natural product; the time of collecting","arabic":"يحصد / حصاد","example":"Workers harvest frankincense by cutting the bark.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS4-V04","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS4-V04","tier":"core"},{"word":"sap","pos":"noun","ipa":"/sæp/","definition":"the liquid inside a plant or a tree","arabic":"عصارة النبات","example":"A milky sap flows out of the cut.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS4-V05","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS4-V05","tier":"core"},{"word":"demand","pos":"noun","ipa":"/dɪˈmænd/","definition":"how much people want to buy something","arabic":"الطلب","example":"Demand for incense was very high.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS4-V06","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS4-V06","tier":"core"},{"word":"credit","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ˈkrɛdɪt/","definition":"to say who wrote or gave information","arabic":"ينسب (المعلومة إلى مصدرها)","example":"Credit the source with 'According to…'.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS4-V07","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS4-V07","tier":"core"}],"explanation":{"text":"To **paraphrase** facts means to give the **same information** in **your own words**. In research writing, we paraphrase so that we **understand** the facts, **avoid copying** and make our writing flow. A paraphrase is usually about the **same length** as the source – it is not a summary.\n\nSteps: (1) **read** the source twice; (2) take **notes** with key words, arrows (→) and symbols (=, +); (3) **cover** the source; (4) **write from your notes**; (5) **check** every fact against the source. **Change:** everyday words (*aromatic → sweet-smelling*) and sentence structure. **Keep exact:** names, numbers, dates, places and special terms.\n\nAlways **credit the source**: *According to an encyclopedia, … / The museum website explains that … / Archaeologists report that …*. Put special terms or exact words in **quotation marks** (*pale lumps called \"tears\"*).","examples":["Source: *an aromatic resin obtained from trees* → ***a sweet-smelling resin that comes from trees*** (words).","Source: *To harvest it, workers make small cuts in the bark.* → ***Workers cut the bark*** (shorter structure).","Source: *A milky sap flows out and, over several weeks, hardens into pale lumps* → ***a milky sap leaks out. After several weeks, the sap turns into hard, pale lumps*** (split + words).","Source: *Because demand was so high, it was one of the most valuable goods* → ***So many people wanted it that it became one of the most valuable goods*** (structure).","Keep exact: ***Boswellia***, ***the Horn of Africa***, ***several weeks***, ***\"tears\"***.","Credit: ***According to an encyclopedia,*** frankincense is…","Notes example: *cut bark → milky sap → several weeks → hard pale lumps = \"tears\"*","Wrong – changed fact ✗: *After several days, the sap turns hard.* (the source says *weeks*)","Wrong – too close ✗: *Frankincense is an aromatic resin obtained from trees of the genus Boswellia.*"],"common_errors":["✗ Changing a number or a date. → ✓ Keep numbers, dates and names exact. (Facts stay the same.)","✗ Changing only one word (patchwriting). → ✓ Change the words AND the structure. (Really your own words.)","✗ Writing notes in full copied sentences. → ✓ Use key words, arrows and symbols. (Short notes.)","✗ No credit: Frankincense comes from Boswellia trees. → ✓ According to an encyclopedia, frankincense comes from Boswellia trees. (Credit the source.)","✗ Changing special terms: the lumps are called 'drops'. → ✓ The lumps are called \"tears.\" (Keep special terms.)","✗ According an encyclopedia, … → ✓ According to an encyclopedia, … (**According to** + source.)","✗ Adding your own opinion: Frankincense smells the best. → ✓ Leave opinions out of a factual paraphrase. (Facts only.)"],"arabic":"إعادة صياغة الحقائق تعني تقديم المعلومات نفسها بكلماتك لتفهمها وتتجنب النسخ، وطولها عادة قريب من طول المصدر، فهي ليست تلخيصًا.\n\nالخطوات: اقرأ المصدر مرتين، ودوّن ملاحظات بالكلمات المفتاحية والأسهم والرموز، ثم غطِّ المصدر واكتب من ملاحظاتك، ثم تحقّق من كل حقيقة.\n\nغيّر الكلمات العامة وتركيب الجمل، وأبقِ الأسماء والأرقام والتواريخ والأماكن والمصطلحات الخاصة كما هي.\n\nانسب المعلومات إلى مصدرها: According to an encyclopedia، وضع المصطلحات الخاصة بين علامتي تنصيص."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:30","narration":"Copying is easy. Paraphrasing shows you really understand. Let's learn how!","on_screen":"Paraphrasing Facts 📝","visual":"A copy machine with a red cross; Ziyad holding a notebook."},{"time":"00:30–01:30","narration":"Frankincense is an aromatic resin obtained from trees of the genus Boswellia…","on_screen":"(source text)","visual":"Boswellia trees on a dry mountain; a worker cuts bark; white sap hardens into 'tears'."},{"time":"01:30–02:30","narration":"Notes, not sentences! Key words, arrows and symbols.","on_screen":"cut bark → milky sap → several weeks → \"tears\"","visual":"Words shrink into a short, neat note card."},{"time":"02:30–03:30","narration":"Cover the source. Write from your notes. Change the words and the structure – but keep names, numbers and special terms exact!","on_screen":"CHANGE: words, structure · KEEP: names, numbers, dates, terms","visual":"Two baskets: CHANGE and KEEP; words fly into the right basket."},{"time":"03:30–04:00","narration":"Finally, credit your source: According to an encyclopedia…","on_screen":"Credit the source ✔","visual":"A hand points from the paragraph back to a book."}],"captions_ar":["النسخ سهل، أما إعادة الصياغة فتُظهر أنك فهمت حقًا. لنتعلم كيف!","اللبان صمغ عطري يُستخرج من أشجار جنس البوسويليا…","ملاحظات لا جمل! كلمات مفتاحية وأسهم ورموز.","غطِّ المصدر واكتب من ملاحظاتك، وغيّر الكلمات والتركيب، لكن أبقِ الأسماء والأرقام والمصطلحات كما هي!","وأخيرًا انسب المعلومات إلى مصدرها: According to an encyclopedia…"],"voice":"Narrator: American English, clear female teacher voice, speed 1.0. Ziyad explains his steps."},"texts":[{"type":"model","title":"From Source to Notes to Paraphrase: Frankincense (by Ziyad)","body":"**Source (encyclopedia entry):** Frankincense is an aromatic resin obtained from trees of the genus *Boswellia*, which grow in the dry mountains of southern Arabia and the Horn of Africa. To harvest it, workers make small cuts in the bark. A milky sap flows out and, over several weeks, hardens into pale lumps known as \"tears.\" In the ancient world, frankincense was burned in temples and used in medicines and perfumes. Because demand was so high, it was one of the most valuable goods traded along the caravan routes.\n\n**Ziyad's notes:** frankincense = sweet-smelling resin · *Boswellia* trees · dry mountains, S. Arabia + Horn of Africa · cut bark → milky sap → several weeks → hard pale lumps = \"tears\" · uses: burned in temples, medicine, perfume · very valuable · caravan routes\n\n**Ziyad's paraphrase:** According to an encyclopedia, frankincense is a sweet-smelling resin that comes from *Boswellia* trees. These trees grow in the dry mountains of southern Arabia and the Horn of Africa. Workers cut the bark, and a milky sap leaks out. After several weeks, the sap turns into hard, pale lumps called \"tears.\" Long ago, people burned frankincense in temples and put it in medicines and perfumes. So many people wanted it that it became one of the most valuable goods carried by caravans.","word_count":206,"audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS4-T1","id":"TXT-RW-L06-U1-LS4-1","genre":"model text","role":"model","difficulty":"700L–850L","glossary":[],"annotations":["**Write from notes:** Ziyad took short notes (key words, arrows, symbols) and then covered the source.","**Keep the facts exact:** names (*Boswellia*, Horn of Africa), times (*several weeks*) and special terms (*\"tears\"*) stay the same.","**Change everyday words:** *aromatic* → *sweet-smelling*; *obtained from* → *comes from*; *flows out* → *leaks out*; *hardens* → *turns into hard lumps*.","**Change the structure:** *Because demand was so high, it was one of the most valuable goods* → *So many people wanted it that it became one of the most valuable goods*.","**Split long sentences:** the first long source sentence became two shorter sentences.","**Credit the source:** *According to an encyclopedia,* tells the reader where the facts came from."]}],"activities":[{"id":"RW-L06-U1-LS4-A1","type":"mcq","stage":"controlled","instructions":"Controlled practice: Read the source, the notes and the paraphrase. Choose the answer.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ المصدر والملاحظات وإعادة الصياغة واختر الإجابة.","items":[{"q":"Where do *Boswellia* trees grow, according to the source?","answer":"in the dry mountains of southern Arabia and the Horn of Africa","explanation":"Source.","explanation_ar":"في جبال جنوب الجزيرة العربية والقرن الأفريقي.","options":["in the dry mountains of southern Arabia and the Horn of Africa","in rainforests of South America","along the Nile only","in snowy mountains"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"How long does the sap take to harden into \"tears\"?","answer":"several weeks","explanation":"Source.","explanation_ar":"عدة أسابيع.","options":["several minutes","several years","one day","several weeks"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Which word did Ziyad use instead of *aromatic*?","answer":"sweet-smelling","explanation":"Changed word.","explanation_ar":"sweet-smelling.","options":["milky","valuable","sweet-smelling","pale"],"dok":1,"skill":"paraphrasing","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Which special term did Ziyad keep in quotation marks?","answer":"\"tears\"","explanation":"Special term.","explanation_ar":"الدموع (tears).","options":["\"bark\"","\"temples\"","\"caravans\"","\"tears\""],"dok":1,"skill":"paraphrasing","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"How did Ziyad credit the source?","answer":"He began with *According to an encyclopedia,*","explanation":"Credit.","explanation_ar":"According to an encyclopedia.","options":["He wrote the author's phone number.","He began with *According to an encyclopedia,*","He did not credit it.","He copied the whole source."],"dok":1,"skill":"crediting sources","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Why are Ziyad's notes written with arrows and short words?","answer":"to avoid copying full sentences and to remember the key facts","explanation":"Notes.","explanation_ar":"لتجنب نسخ الجمل.","options":["to avoid copying full sentences and to remember the key facts","because he had no time to write","to make the notes longer","because arrows look nice"],"dok":2,"skill":"note-taking","stage":"controlled"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U1-LS4-A2","type":"mcq","stage":"guided","instructions":"Guided practice: Paraphrasing facts. Choose.","instructions_ar":"إعادة صياغة الحقائق: اختر.","items":[{"q":"Source: *Hegra has 111 monumental tombs.* Which paraphrase is CORRECT?","answer":"There are 111 huge tombs at Hegra.","explanation":"Keep the number.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["There are 111 huge tombs at Hegra.","Hegra has about 100 tombs.","Hegra has 11 monumental tombs.","Hegra has many small houses."],"dok":2,"skill":"accuracy","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Source: *In 106 CE, the Roman Empire took control of the Nabataean kingdom.* Which paraphrase is BEST?","answer":"The Nabataean kingdom came under Roman rule in 106 CE.","explanation":"Paraphrase.","explanation_ar":"الأفضل.","options":["In 106 CE, the Roman Empire took control of the Nabataean kingdom.","The Nabataeans controlled Rome in 106 CE.","The Nabataean kingdom came under Roman rule in 106 CE.","The Romans visited Hegra a long time ago."],"dok":2,"skill":"paraphrasing","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which of these should you KEEP exactly when you paraphrase facts about Dedan?","answer":"the name *Dedan* and the dates","explanation":"Keep.","explanation_ar":"الأسماء والتواريخ.","options":["every adjective","the name *Dedan* and the dates","the order of every word","the writer's sentence length"],"dok":2,"skill":"paraphrasing","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which paraphrase about frankincense harvest is *patchwriting* (too close to the source)?","answer":"To harvest it, workers make small cuts in the tree bark.","explanation":"Too close.","explanation_ar":"قريبة جدًا من المصدر.","options":["Workers cut the bark to collect it.","To harvest it, workers make small cuts in the tree bark.","The harvest begins when workers slice into the bark.","People gather it by cutting the bark."],"dok":3,"skill":"avoiding copying","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which sentence about the museum website uses *According to* correctly?","answer":"According to the museum website, the tomb is two thousand years old.","explanation":"According to + source.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["According the museum website, the tomb is two thousand years old.","According to of the museum website, the tomb is two thousand years old.","Accord to the museum website, the tomb is two thousand years old.","According to the museum website, the tomb is two thousand years old."],"dok":2,"skill":"crediting sources","stage":"guided"},{"q":"A paraphrase says *Frankincense comes from palm trees.* What is the problem?","answer":"It changes a fact – the source says Boswellia trees.","explanation":"Accuracy.","explanation_ar":"غيّر الحقيقة.","options":["It is too short.","It uses quotation marks.","It changes a fact – the source says Boswellia trees.","It credits the source."],"dok":3,"skill":"accuracy","stage":"guided"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U1-LS4-A3","type":"gap_fill","stage":"freer","instructions":"Freer practice: Paraphrase the facts. Use the word bank.","instructions_ar":"أعد صياغة الحقائق من بنك الكلمات.","items":[{"q":"*aromatic* resin → a ________ resin","answer":"sweet-smelling","explanation":"Everyday word.","explanation_ar":"ذو رائحة زكية.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"*obtained from* trees → ________ from trees","answer":"comes","explanation":"Simpler verb.","explanation_ar":"يأتي.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"the sap *flows out* → the sap ________ out","answer":"leaks","explanation":"Synonym.","explanation_ar":"يتسرّب.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"*Because demand was so high* → So many people ________ it that…","answer":"wanted","explanation":"New structure.","explanation_ar":"أرادوه.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"________ to an encyclopedia, frankincense is very old.","answer":"According","explanation":"Credit.","explanation_ar":"وفقًا لـ.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Notes: cut bark ________ milky sap (use a symbol for 'leads to')","answer":"→","explanation":"Symbol.","explanation_ar":"سهم.","stage":"freer"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U1-LS4-A4","type":"open","stage":"production","instructions":"Writing: Take notes and paraphrase.","instructions_ar":"دوّن الملاحظات وأعد الصياغة.","items":[{"q":"Take notes in key words and symbols: *The Nabataeans cut dozens of wells deep into the rock at Hegra to reach underground water.*","answer":"Nabataeans → dozens of wells → cut deep in rock → Hegra → underground water","explanation":"Notes.","explanation_ar":"ملاحظات.","dok":2,"skill":"note-taking","stage":"production"},{"q":"Paraphrase from your notes and credit the source (the Hegra article).","answer":"According to the Hegra article, the Nabataeans dug dozens of deep wells through the rock so that they could reach water under the ground.","explanation":"Paraphrase.","explanation_ar":"إعادة صياغة.","dok":2,"skill":"paraphrasing","stage":"production"},{"q":"Paraphrase: *Qasr al-Farid was carved from a single huge rock, but its lower part was never completed.*","answer":"Qasr al-Farid was cut out of one giant rock, and workers never finished its bottom part.","explanation":"Paraphrase.","explanation_ar":"إعادة صياغة.","dok":3,"skill":"paraphrasing","stage":"production"},{"q":"Check this paraphrase and fix TWO problems: *Hegra became a UNESCO site in 2018. I think it is the best place in the world.*","answer":"Hegra became a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2008. (I corrected the date and removed the opinion.)","explanation":"Revise.","explanation_ar":"حسّن.","dok":3,"skill":"revising","stage":"production"}]}],"game":{"name":"Change or Keep?","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["On this screen: answer the lesson questions as fast as you can.","In class: Sorting machine: words from a source sentence fall down the screen; the child drags names, numbers, dates and terms into KEEP and everyday words into CHANGE, then picks the best paraphrase. A wrong number sets off a 'fact alarm'.","A team draws a source card and cuts it into word chips.","They sort the chips into CHANGE and KEEP baskets.","They write a paraphrase on a mini whiteboard.","The 'fact checker' team compares it with the source.","Accurate paraphrase = 3 points; every changed fact = minus 1 point."],"data":[]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"What does *paraphrase facts* mean?","answer":"give the same facts in your own words","explanation":"Definition.","explanation_ar":"إعادة صياغة الحقائق.","options":["give the same facts in your own words","copy the facts exactly","add your opinion to the facts","make the facts shorter and change them"],"dok":1,"skill":"paraphrasing"},{"q":"What is a *source*?","answer":"a book, a website or a person that gives information","explanation":"Vocabulary.","explanation_ar":"مصدر.","options":["a book, a website or a person that gives information","a kind of tree","a short note","a quotation mark"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary"},{"q":"*Workers **harvest** frankincense in the hot months.* *Harvest* means ___.","answer":"collect","explanation":"Context.","explanation_ar":"يجمعون.","options":["burn","sell","plant","collect"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary in context"},{"q":"What should good notes look like?","answer":"key words, arrows and symbols","explanation":"Notes.","explanation_ar":"كلمات مفتاحية ورموز.","options":["full sentences copied from the source","a drawing only","your opinions","key words, arrows and symbols"],"dok":1,"skill":"note-taking"},{"q":"Which sentence about the encyclopedia is correct?","answer":"According to the encyclopedia, the trees grow in dry mountains.","explanation":"According to.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["According the encyclopedia, the trees grow in dry mountains.","According to the encyclopedia, the trees grow in dry mountains.","According at the encyclopedia, the trees grow in dry mountains.","According to the encyclopedia the trees, grow in dry mountains."],"dok":2,"skill":"crediting sources"},{"q":"Which should you NOT change when you paraphrase?","answer":"the date *106 CE*","explanation":"Keep dates.","explanation_ar":"التاريخ.","options":["the word *obtained*","the sentence order","the date *106 CE*","the word *aromatic*"],"dok":2,"skill":"paraphrasing"},{"q":"Source: *The sap hardens over several weeks.* Which paraphrase is accurate?","answer":"After a few weeks, the sap becomes hard.","explanation":"Accuracy.","explanation_ar":"دقيقة.","options":["After a few minutes, the sap becomes hard.","The sap never becomes hard.","After a few weeks, the sap becomes hard.","The sap hardens over several years."],"dok":2,"skill":"accuracy"},{"q":"Why should you cover the source while you write?","answer":"so you write from your notes in your own words","explanation":"Strategy.","explanation_ar":"لتكتب بكلماتك.","options":["so you forget the facts","so you write from your notes in your own words","so the paragraph is longer","so you can add new facts"],"dok":2,"skill":"paraphrasing process"},{"q":"How is a paraphrase DIFFERENT from a summary?","answer":"A paraphrase keeps all the facts and is about the same length; a summary keeps only the main ideas and is shorter.","explanation":"Compare.","explanation_ar":"إعادة الصياغة تحفظ كل الحقائق.","options":["They are exactly the same.","A paraphrase is always shorter.","A paraphrase keeps all the facts and is about the same length; a summary keeps only the main ideas and is shorter.","A summary keeps every detail."],"dok":3,"skill":"paraphrasing"},{"q":"Why is it important to credit your source?","answer":"It shows where the facts came from and respects the writer's work.","explanation":"Academic honesty.","explanation_ar":"يحترم عمل الكاتب ويوضح المصدر.","options":["It makes the text funny.","It lets you copy without changes.","It hides your mistakes.","It shows where the facts came from and respects the writer's work."],"dok":3,"skill":"academic honesty"}],"images":[{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS4-V01","prompt":"Picture for the word 'source': a book, a website and an expert"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS4-V02","prompt":"Picture for the word 'notes': a notebook with arrows and short words"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS4-V03","prompt":"Picture for the word 'aromatic': smoke curling from an incense burner"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS4-V04","prompt":"Picture for the word 'harvest': a worker cutting the bark of a tree"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS4-V05","prompt":"Picture for the word 'sap': white drops on a tree trunk"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS4-V06","prompt":"Picture for the word 'demand': many hands reaching for one jar"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS4-V07","prompt":"Picture for the word 'credit': a pointing hand toward a book"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS4-01","prompt":"Harvesting frankincense from a Boswellia tree."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS4-02","prompt":"The three steps of paraphrasing."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS4-03","prompt":"Sorting words to change and keep."}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS4-V01","script":"source. Always check your source."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS4-V02","script":"notes. Take notes in key words."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS4-V03","script":"aromatic. Frankincense is an aromatic resin."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS4-V04","script":"harvest. Workers harvest frankincense by cutting the bark."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS4-V05","script":"sap. A milky sap flows out of the cut."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS4-V06","script":"demand. Demand for incense was very high."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS4-V07","script":"credit. Credit the source with 'According to…'."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS4-T1","script":"**Source (encyclopedia entry):** Frankincense is an aromatic resin obtained from trees of the genus *Boswellia*, which grow in the dry mountains of southern Arabia and the Horn of Africa. To harvest it, workers make small cuts in the bark. A milky sap flows out and, over several weeks, hardens into pale lumps known as \"tears.\" In the ancient world, frankincense was burned in temples and used in medicines and perfumes. Because demand was so high, it was one of the most valuable goods traded along the caravan routes. **Ziyad's notes:** frankincense = sweet-smelling resin · *Boswellia* trees · dry mountains, S. Arabia + Horn of Africa · cut bark → milky sap → several weeks → hard pale lumps = \"tears\" · uses: burned in temples, medicine, perfume · very valuable · caravan routes **Ziyad's paraphrase:** According to an encyclopedia, frankincense is a sweet-smelling resin that comes from *Boswellia* trees. These trees grow in the dry mountains of southern Arabia and the Horn of Africa. Workers cut the bark, and a milky sap leaks out. After several weeks, the sap turns into hard, pale lumps called \"tears.\" Long ago, people burned frankincense in temples and put it in medicines and perfumes. So many people wanted it that it became one of the most valuable goods carried by caravans."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS4-VID","script":"Copying is easy. Paraphrasing shows you really understand. Let's learn how! Frankincense is an aromatic resin obtained from trees of the genus Boswellia… Notes, not sentences! Key words, arrows and symbols. Cover the source. Write from your notes. Change the words and the structure – but keep names, numbers and special terms exact! Finally, credit your source: According to an encyclopedia…"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U1-LS5","title":"Writing Workshop: A three-paragraph report on an ancient site","lesson_type":"writing_workshop","objectives":["plan a three-paragraph report on an ancient site in Saudi Arabia from a fact file with the Report Planner","write 150–200 words with a clear introduction, a body of paraphrased facts and a conclusion","revise, edit and publish the report in a class 'Heritage Trail' booklet"],"objectives_ar":["التخطيط لتقرير من ثلاث فقرات عن موقع أثري في المملكة من ملف حقائق.","كتابة 150–200 كلمة بمقدمة واضحة ومتن من الحقائق المعاد صياغتها وخاتمة.","تحسين التقرير وتدقيقه ونشره في كتيّب «مسار التراث» الصفي."],"success_criteria":["My introduction has a hook and tells what, where and when, and why the site matters.","My body paragraph explains the history and special features with exact, paraphrased facts and a credit.","My conclusion tells about the site today and why we should protect it (150–200 words in total)."],"success_criteria_ar":["مقدمتي فيها جملة جذب وتذكر ماهية الموقع وموقعه وزمنه وأهميته.","فقرة المتن تشرح التاريخ والسمات الخاصة بحقائق دقيقة معاد صياغتها مع نسبتها إلى المصدر.","خاتمتي تذكر حال الموقع اليوم وسبب حمايته (150–200 كلمة إجمالًا)."],"map_area":"Language Usage: Write, Revise Texts for Purpose and Audience (cross-strand)","vocabulary":[{"word":"heritage","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈhɛrɪtɪdʒ/","definition":"the history, buildings and traditions that people receive from the past","arabic":"تراث","example":"Diriyah is part of our national heritage.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS5-V01","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS5-V01","tier":"core"},{"word":"district","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈdɪstrɪkt/","definition":"an area of a town or a country","arabic":"حيّ / منطقة","example":"At-Turaif is a historic district in Diriyah.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS5-V02","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS5-V02","tier":"core"},{"word":"mud brick","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈmʌd ˌbrɪk/","definition":"a brick made of mud and straw and dried in the sun","arabic":"طوب طيني (لَبِن)","example":"The palaces were built of mud brick.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS5-V03","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS5-V03","tier":"core"},{"word":"restore","pos":"verb","ipa":"/rɪˈstɔr/","definition":"to repair an old building so it looks as it did before","arabic":"يرمّم","example":"Experts restored Salwa Palace.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS5-V04","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS5-V04","tier":"core"},{"word":"rock art","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈrɑk ˌɑrt/","definition":"pictures carved or painted on rocks long ago","arabic":"فن صخري / نقوش صخرية","example":"The rock art in Jubbah shows camels and people.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS5-V05","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS5-V05","tier":"core"},{"word":"oasis","pos":"noun","ipa":"/oʊˈeɪsɪs/","definition":"a place in the desert with water and trees (plural: oases)","arabic":"واحة","example":"Al-Ahsa is one of the largest oases in the world.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS5-V06","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS5-V06","tier":"core"},{"word":"World Heritage Site","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌwɝld ˈhɛrɪtɪdʒ ˌsaɪt/","definition":"a place that UNESCO lists as important for all people to protect","arabic":"موقع تراث عالمي","example":"At-Turaif became a World Heritage Site in 2010.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS5-V07","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS5-V07","tier":"core"}],"explanation":{"text":"A **report** gives **facts** about one topic in an organized way. At this level, a report has **three paragraphs**: an **introduction**, a **body** and a **conclusion**. Each paragraph has **one job**, and each starts on a new line.\n\n**Paragraph 1 – Introduction:** a **hook** (*Imagine…*, a question or an amazing fact) + **what** the site is, **where** it is, **when** it was built or used, and **why it matters**. **Paragraph 2 – Body:** the **history** in **chronological order** and the **special features** (buildings, carvings, water) – **paraphrased** facts with exact names, numbers and dates, and a **credit** (*According to the fact file,*). **Paragraph 3 – Conclusion:** the site **today** and **why we should protect it**, ending with a strong final thought.\n\nUse **time signals** (*In the fifteenth century, Later, In 1818, Today*) and **linking words** (*because, so, However*). Write in the **past simple** for history and the **present simple** for today. Keep it **150–200 words**.","examples":["Hook: ***Imagine a city of golden mud-brick palaces rising beside a green valley.***","What + where: ***This is At-Turaif, a historic district in Diriyah, on the edge of Riyadh.***","Why it matters + credit: ***According to the fact file, it was the capital of the first Saudi state, and in 2010 it became a World Heritage Site.***","History (time signal): ***At-Turaif was founded in the fifteenth century beside Wadi Hanifah.***","Feature + cause: ***Thick mud-brick walls and small triangle-shaped openings kept the rooms cool in the summer heat.***","Event: ***In 1818, the town was attacked and largely destroyed.***","Today (present simple): ***Today, careful restoration has brought At-Turaif back to life, and visitors walk through its lanes and museums.***","Why protect it: ***It reminds us that, long before skyscrapers, people here built clever homes from the earth itself.***","Closing: ***Protecting places like At-Turaif helps us remember where we come from.***"],"common_errors":["✗ One long paragraph with everything mixed. → ✓ Three paragraphs: introduction, body, conclusion. (One job per paragraph.)","✗ Copying the fact file notes. → ✓ Paraphrase the notes into full sentences. (Own words.)","✗ Changing a date: At-Turaif became a site in 2012. → ✓ At-Turaif became a World Heritage Site in 2010. (Keep dates exact.)","✗ History in the present: It is founded in the 15th century. → ✓ It was founded in the fifteenth century. (History → past simple (passive *was founded*).)","✗ Events out of order. → ✓ Use time signals in chronological order. (Oldest first.)","✗ No credit for the facts. → ✓ According to the fact file, … (Credit the source.)","✗ A conclusion with new history facts. → ✓ Conclusion = today + why protect it. (No new history facts.)"],"arabic":"التقرير يعرض حقائق عن موضوع واحد بشكل منظم، وفي هذا المستوى يتكون من ثلاث فقرات: مقدمة ومتن وخاتمة، ولكل فقرة وظيفة واحدة.\n\nالمقدمة: جملة جذب، وماهية الموقع ومكانه وزمنه وسبب أهميته. المتن: التاريخ بترتيب زمني والسمات الخاصة بحقائق معاد صياغتها مع نسبتها إلى المصدر. الخاتمة: حال الموقع اليوم وسبب حمايته مع فكرة ختامية قوية.\n\nاستخدم مؤشرات الزمن وكلمات الربط، والماضي البسيط للتاريخ والمضارع البسيط للحاضر، والتزم بطول 150–200 كلمة.\n\nانتبه: أبقِ الأسماء والأرقام والتواريخ كما هي في ملف الحقائق."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:30","narration":"Lions carved in cliffs, camels drawn ten thousand years ago, palaces of mud… Which site will you report on?","on_screen":"My Ancient Site Report 🏺","visual":"Quick shots of Dadan's Lion Tombs, Jubbah rock art, At-Turaif and Al-Ahsa palms."},{"time":"00:30–01:20","narration":"Choose a fact file: Dadan, the rock art of Ha'il, At-Turaif or Al-Ahsa Oasis.","on_screen":"Fact Files A–D","visual":"Four illustrated cards slide onto a map of Saudi Arabia."},{"time":"01:20–02:30","narration":"Imagine a city of golden mud-brick palaces rising beside a green valley…","on_screen":"(model report)","visual":"Animated At-Turaif: palaces, triangle-shaped openings, Salwa Palace, then visitors today."},{"time":"02:30–03:30","narration":"Paragraph 1: hook, what, where, when, why. Paragraph 2: history and features. Paragraph 3: today and why protect it!","on_screen":"P1 Introduction · P2 Body · P3 Conclusion","visual":"Three paragraph blocks stack like mud bricks."},{"time":"03:30–04:00","narration":"Paraphrase, keep the facts exact, credit your source – and add your page to the Heritage Trail!","on_screen":"Heritage Trail 📘","visual":"Student pages form a trail across a map."}],"captions_ar":["أسود منحوتة في الجروف، وجمال رُسمت قبل عشرة آلاف عام، وقصور من الطين… عن أي موقع ستكتب تقريرك؟","اختر ملف حقائق: دادان، أو الفن الصخري في حائل، أو حي الطريف، أو واحة الأحساء.","تخيّل مدينة من قصور الطين الذهبية ترتفع بجانب وادٍ أخضر…","الفقرة الأولى: جذب وماهية ومكان وزمن وأهمية. الثانية: التاريخ والسمات. الثالثة: اليوم وسبب الحماية!","أعد الصياغة، وحافظ على دقة الحقائق، وانسبها إلى مصدرها، وأضف صفحتك إلى مسار التراث!"],"voice":"Narrator: American English, friendly male guide voice, speed 1.0. A student reads the model report."},"texts":[{"type":"model","title":"Ancient Sites of Saudi Arabia: Fact Files","body":"**File A – Dadan (Dedan), AlUla** · Where: a green oasis valley near AlUla, northwest Saudi Arabia · When: center of the kingdoms of Dadan and Lihyan more than 2,500 years ago · Why important: traders on the incense route stopped here; inscriptions in the ancient Dadanitic script · Famous feature: the Lion Tombs – tombs cut high into a red sandstone cliff, some with lions carved beside them · Today: archaeologists are still digging here\n\n**File B – Rock Art of the Ha'il Region (Jubbah and Shuwaymis)** · Where: Ha'il region, northern Saudi Arabia · When: thousands of carvings made over about 10,000 years · What: pictures of people, camels, ibex and horses cut into rock · Amazing fact: Jubbah once stood beside an ancient lake, so people and animals lived where there is now desert · Today: UNESCO World Heritage Site (2015); protected by fences and guards from damage\n\n**File C – At-Turaif District, Diriyah** · Where: Diriyah, on the edge of Riyadh, beside Wadi Hanifah · Founded: 15th century · Importance: capital of the first Saudi state (18th century) · Buildings: Najdi-style mud-brick palaces with thick walls, small triangle-shaped openings and carved wooden doors that keep rooms cool; Salwa Palace = the largest, home of the ruling family · Event: attacked and largely destroyed in 1818 · Today: UNESCO World Heritage Site (2010); restored, with museums open to visitors\n\n**File D – Al-Ahsa Oasis** · Where: Eastern Province · What: one of the largest oases in the world, with more than 2.5 million date palms · Water: natural springs, canals and wells · History: people have lived here for thousands of years; the Jawatha Mosque is one of the oldest mosques in Arabia · Today: UNESCO World Heritage Site (2018)","word_count":269,"audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS5-T1","id":"TXT-RW-L06-U1-LS5-1","genre":"fact files","role":"model","difficulty":"700L–850L","glossary":[],"annotations":["Choose **one file**. You will write a **report** with facts, not a story.","**Paraphrase** the notes in your own words. Keep names, numbers and dates **exact**, and **credit** the fact file.","Plan **three paragraphs**: (1) introduction – hook + what, where, when and why it matters; (2) body – history and special features; (3) conclusion – the site today and why we should protect it."]}],"activities":[{"id":"RW-L06-U1-LS5-A1","type":"mcq","stage":"controlled","instructions":"Controlled practice: Read the fact files and the model. Choose the answer.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ ملفات الحقائق والنموذج واختر الإجابة.","items":[{"q":"What famous feature can you see at Dadan?","answer":"the Lion Tombs cut into a red sandstone cliff","explanation":"File A.","explanation_ar":"مقابر الأسود.","options":["2.5 million date palms","Salwa Palace","the Lion Tombs cut into a red sandstone cliff","carvings of horses made 10,000 years ago"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Why could people and animals live at Jubbah long ago?","answer":"It once stood beside an ancient lake.","explanation":"File B.","explanation_ar":"كانت بجوار بحيرة قديمة.","options":["It once stood beside an ancient lake.","It had a large river.","It was near the Red Sea.","It had many wells cut into rock."],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"When did At-Turaif become a World Heritage Site?","answer":"in 2010","explanation":"File C.","explanation_ar":"عام 2010.","options":["in 2010","in 2015","in 2018","in 1818"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"How many date palms grow in Al-Ahsa Oasis, according to File D?","answer":"more than 2.5 million","explanation":"File D.","explanation_ar":"أكثر من مليونين ونصف.","options":["about 2,500","more than 2.5 million","exactly 1 million","fewer than 100,000"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Why did the Najdi-style buildings stay cool in summer?","answer":"Thick mud-brick walls, small openings and wooden doors kept out the heat.","explanation":"Cause.","explanation_ar":"الجدران السميكة والفتحات الصغيرة.","options":["They had air conditioners.","They were built underground.","They were made of glass.","Thick mud-brick walls, small openings and wooden doors kept out the heat."],"dok":2,"skill":"cause and effect","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"In the model, which paragraph tells about the site TODAY?","answer":"Paragraph 3 – the conclusion","explanation":"Organization.","explanation_ar":"الفقرة الثالثة.","options":["Paragraph 1 – the introduction","Paragraph 3 – the conclusion","Paragraph 2 – the body","none of them"],"dok":1,"skill":"organization","stage":"controlled"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U1-LS5-A2","type":"mcq","stage":"guided","instructions":"Guided practice: Plan your report. Choose.","instructions_ar":"خطّط لتقريرك: اختر.","items":[{"q":"Which is the BEST hook for a report on the rock art of the Ha'il region?","answer":"Imagine drawing a camel on a rock – and people still seeing it thousands of years later!","explanation":"Hook.","explanation_ar":"الأفضل.","options":["This is my report about rocks.","Rock art is a thing.","Ha'il is a region.","Imagine drawing a camel on a rock – and people still seeing it thousands of years later!"],"dok":1,"skill":"hooks","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which sentence belongs in the BODY paragraph of a report on Dadan?","answer":"More than 2,500 years ago, Dadan was the center of the kingdoms of Dadan and Lihyan.","explanation":"Body = history and features.","explanation_ar":"مكانها المتن.","options":["Imagine lions guarding tombs high in a red cliff.","Today, archaeologists are still digging, and we should protect the site.","More than 2,500 years ago, Dadan was the center of the kingdoms of Dadan and Lihyan.","Protecting Dadan helps us remember our past."],"dok":2,"skill":"organization","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which sentence about At-Turaif's history uses the correct verb form?","answer":"At-Turaif was founded in the fifteenth century.","explanation":"Past passive.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["At-Turaif is founded in the fifteenth century.","At-Turaif was founded in the fifteenth century.","At-Turaif founded in the fifteenth century.","At-Turaif was find in the fifteenth century."],"dok":2,"skill":"verb tense","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which sentence is a correct PARAPHRASE of File D's water notes?","answer":"Natural springs, canals and wells bring water to the oasis.","explanation":"Paraphrase.","explanation_ar":"إعادة صياغة صحيحة.","options":["Water: natural springs, canals and wells.","The oasis has no water.","Natural springs, canals and wells bring water to the oasis.","Al-Ahsa gets water from the Red Sea."],"dok":2,"skill":"paraphrasing","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which order of events for At-Turaif is chronological?","answer":"founded → capital of the first Saudi state → attacked in 1818 → World Heritage Site","explanation":"Time order.","explanation_ar":"الترتيب الزمني.","options":["founded → capital of the first Saudi state → attacked in 1818 → World Heritage Site","World Heritage Site → founded → attacked → capital","attacked → founded → capital → World Heritage Site","capital → World Heritage Site → founded → attacked"],"dok":2,"skill":"chronological order","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which is the strongest CLOSING sentence for a report on Al-Ahsa Oasis?","answer":"Every palm in Al-Ahsa is a living link to the people who farmed here thousands of years ago.","explanation":"Closing.","explanation_ar":"أقوى خاتمة.","options":["Al-Ahsa has palms.","That is my report.","I like dates.","Every palm in Al-Ahsa is a living link to the people who farmed here thousands of years ago."],"dok":3,"skill":"conclusions","stage":"guided"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U1-LS5-A3","type":"organizer","stage":"freer","instructions":"Organizer: Plan your report with the Report Planner.","instructions_ar":"خطط لتقريرك بمخطط التقرير.","items":[{"q":"Hook and introduction notes (what, where, when, why it matters).","answer":"Hook: lions carved in a red cliff! Dadan – oasis near AlUla – center of the kingdoms of Dadan and Lihyan, 2,500+ years ago – on the incense route","explanation":"Introduction.","explanation_ar":"المقدمة.","dok":2,"skill":"planning","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Body notes: history in time order and special features.","answer":"kingdoms of Dadan and Lihyan → traders on the incense route stopped → inscriptions in Dadanitic script; Lion Tombs cut high in red sandstone, some with carved lions","explanation":"Body.","explanation_ar":"المتن.","dok":2,"skill":"planning","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Conclusion notes: today and why protect it.","answer":"archaeologists still digging → new discoveries; protect it = learn about the first kingdoms of Arabia","explanation":"Conclusion.","explanation_ar":"الخاتمة.","dok":2,"skill":"planning","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Write your source credit sentence.","answer":"According to the fact file, Dadan was the center of two ancient kingdoms more than 2,500 years ago.","explanation":"Credit.","explanation_ar":"النسبة إلى المصدر.","dok":2,"skill":"crediting sources","stage":"freer"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U1-LS5-A4","type":"writing","stage":"production","instructions":"Writing: Write, revise, edit and publish your report.","instructions_ar":"اكتب تقريرك وحسّنه ودققه وانشره.","items":[{"q":"Write Paragraph 1: the introduction.","answer":"Imagine lions carved high in a red cliff, guarding tombs for more than 2,500 years. This is Dadan, an ancient oasis city near AlUla. According to the fact file, it was the center of the kingdoms of Dadan and Lihyan.","explanation":"Introduction.","explanation_ar":"المقدمة.","dok":2,"skill":"writing","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write Paragraph 2: the body (history and features).","answer":"Dadan grew important because traders on the incense route stopped in its green valley. The people wrote inscriptions in the Dadanitic script. Its most famous feature is the Lion Tombs, which were cut high into a red sandstone cliff. Some of them have lions carved beside them.","explanation":"Body.","explanation_ar":"المتن.","dok":2,"skill":"writing","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write Paragraph 3: the conclusion.","answer":"Today, archaeologists are still digging at Dadan and making new discoveries. Protecting it helps us learn about some of the first kingdoms of Arabia.","explanation":"Conclusion.","explanation_ar":"الخاتمة.","dok":2,"skill":"writing","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write one sentence where you changed copied notes into a paraphrase (before → after).","answer":"Before: Famous feature: the Lion Tombs – tombs cut high into a red sandstone cliff. After: Its most famous feature is the Lion Tombs, which were cut high into a red sandstone cliff.","explanation":"Revision.","explanation_ar":"تحسين.","dok":3,"skill":"revising","stage":"production","_w":{"checklist":[],"min":null,"max":null}}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U1-LS5-ORG","type":"organizer","organizer":"Report Planner","instructions":"Organizer: Use one fact file. Write short notes in your own words. Keep names, numbers and dates exact.","items":[{"q":"Hook","answer":"Imagine golden mud-brick palaces beside a green valley."},{"q":"Introduction: what, where, when, why it matters","answer":"At-Turaif, historic district, Diriyah, edge of Riyadh; capital of the first Saudi state; World Heritage Site 2010"},{"q":"Body: history in time order","answer":"founded 15th century by Wadi Hanifah → capital of first Saudi state 18th century → attacked, largely destroyed 1818"},{"q":"Body: special features (buildings, carvings, water)","answer":"Najdi style: thick mud-brick walls, triangle-shaped openings, carved wooden doors keep rooms cool; Salwa Palace = largest"},{"q":"Conclusion: the site today + why protect it","answer":"restored; museums and visitors; reminds us of clever building from earth; protect = remember our roots"},{"q":"Source credit","answer":"According to the fact file, …"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U1-LS5-WR","type":"writing","instructions":"Writing: Choose one fact file (Dadan, the Rock Art of the Ha'il Region, At-Turaif or Al-Ahsa Oasis). Write a three-paragraph report (150–200 words). Paragraph 1: a hook and what, where and when, and why the site matters. Paragraph 2: its history in time order and its special features, with paraphrased facts and a credit. Paragraph 3: the site today, why we should protect it and a closing sentence. Choose one fact file (Dadan, the Rock Art of the Ha'il Region, At-Turaif or Al-Ahsa Oasis). Write a three-paragraph report (150–200 words). Paragraph 1: a hook and what, where and when, and why the site matters. Paragraph 2: its history in time order and its special features, with paraphrased facts and a credit. Paragraph 3: the site today, why we should protect it and a closing sentence.","instructions_ar":"اختر ملف حقائق (دادان، أو الفن الصخري في منطقة حائل، أو حي الطريف، أو واحة الأحساء)، واكتب تقريرًا من ثلاث فقرات (150–200 كلمة). الفقرة الأولى: جملة جذب وماهية الموقع ومكانه وزمنه وأهميته. الثانية: تاريخه بترتيب زمني وسماته الخاصة بحقائق معاد صياغتها مع نسبتها إلى المصدر. الثالثة: حال الموقع اليوم وسبب حمايته مع جملة ختامية.","prompt":"Choose one fact file (Dadan, the Rock Art of the Ha'il Region, At-Turaif or Al-Ahsa Oasis). Write a three-paragraph report (150–200 words). Paragraph 1: a hook and what, where and when, and why the site matters. Paragraph 2: its history in time order and its special features, with paraphrased facts and a credit. Paragraph 3: the site today, why we should protect it and a closing sentence.","checklist":["My report has three paragraphs: introduction, body and conclusion.","My hook makes the reader want to read on.","My introduction tells what, where, when and why the site matters.","My body tells the history in time order with time signals.","I paraphrased the facts and kept names, numbers and dates exact.","I credited the source (According to the fact file, …).","My conclusion tells about the site today and why we should protect it.","I used the past simple for history, the present simple for today and 150–200 words, and I checked spelling and punctuation."],"rubric_id":"RW-RUBRIC-5T","min_words":150,"max_words":220,"organizer":"Report Planner (hook – introduction – history – features – today and protect – source credit)","items":[{"q":"Choose one fact file (Dadan, the Rock Art of the Ha'il Region, At-Turaif or Al-Ahsa Oasis). Write a three-paragraph report (150–200 words). Paragraph 1: a hook and what, where and when, and why the site matters. Paragraph 2: its history in time order and its special features, with paraphrased facts and a credit. Paragraph 3: the site today, why we should protect it and a closing sentence.","answer":"Imagine a city of golden mud-brick palaces rising beside a green valley. This is At-Turaif, a historic district in Diriyah, on the edge of Riyadh. According to the fact file, it was the capital of the first Saudi state, and in 2010 it became a World Heritage Site. (new paragraph) At-Turaif was founded in the fifteenth century beside Wadi Hanifah, where farmers could find water. In the eighteenth century, it became the capital of the first Saudi state and grew into an important center. Its builders used the Najdi style. Thick mud-brick walls, small triangle-shaped openings and carved wooden doors kept the rooms cool in the summer heat. The largest building, Salwa Palace, was the home of the ruling family. In 1818, the town was attacked and largely destroyed. (new paragraph) Today, careful restoration has brought At-Turaif back to life, and visitors walk through its lanes and museums. It reminds us that, long before skyscrapers, people here built clever homes from the earth itself. Protecting places like At-Turaif helps us remember where we come from.","_w":{"checklist":["My report has three paragraphs: introduction, body and conclusion.","My hook makes the reader want to read on.","My introduction tells what, where, when and why the site matters.","My body tells the history in time order with time signals.","I paraphrased the facts and kept names, numbers and dates exact.","I credited the source (According to the fact file, …).","My conclusion tells about the site today and why we should protect it.","I used the past simple for history, the present simple for today and 150–200 words, and I checked spelling and punctuation."],"min":150,"max":220}}]}],"game":{"name":"Heritage Trail Quiz","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["On this screen: answer the lesson questions as fast as you can.","In class: Heritage trail: questions about the four sites appear on a map; the child taps the right site, answers the question and earns a 'heritage stamp'. Answers that copy the fact file word for word get a 'say it your way' challenge.","Each 'reporter' writes two questions from his or her report.","Teams take turns answering other reporters' questions.","After answering, the team pins the site on the map.","Wrong facts or dates lose a point.","Correct answer = 2 points; correct map pin = 1 point."],"data":[]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"How many paragraphs does your ancient-site report have?","answer":"three","explanation":"Organization.","explanation_ar":"ثلاث.","options":["one","three","two","five"],"dok":1,"skill":"organization"},{"q":"What is *heritage*?","answer":"the history, buildings and traditions we receive from the past","explanation":"Vocabulary.","explanation_ar":"التراث.","options":["the history, buildings and traditions we receive from the past","a kind of desert plant","a new shopping mall","a type of camel"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary"},{"q":"*Experts **restored** the old palace.* *Restored* means ___.","answer":"repaired it so it looks as it did before","explanation":"Context.","explanation_ar":"رمّموه.","options":["knocked it down","painted it a new color","sold it","repaired it so it looks as it did before"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary in context"},{"q":"Which belongs in the INTRODUCTION of a report?","answer":"a hook and what, where and when","explanation":"Introduction.","explanation_ar":"جملة جذب وماهية ومكان وزمن.","options":["the site today and why to protect it","a hook and what, where and when","a list of your favorite sites","a new story"],"dok":1,"skill":"organization"},{"q":"Which sentence about Salwa Palace is correct?","answer":"Salwa Palace was the home of the ruling family.","explanation":"was + noun.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["Salwa Palace were the home of the ruling family.","Salwa Palace is was the home of the ruling family.","Salwa Palace was the home of the ruling family.","Salwa Palace being the home of the ruling family."],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar"},{"q":"Which time signal BEST introduces the last event in a site's history?","answer":"Today,","explanation":"Time signal.","explanation_ar":"Today.","options":["Once upon a time,","Today,","First of all,","In the beginning,"],"dok":2,"skill":"time signals"},{"q":"A report says *Al-Ahsa has about 2,500 palms.* What is the problem?","answer":"It changes the number – the fact file says more than 2.5 million.","explanation":"Accuracy.","explanation_ar":"غيّر الرقم.","options":["It changes the number – the fact file says more than 2.5 million.","It is too long.","It uses the present simple.","It credits the source."],"dok":2,"skill":"accuracy"},{"q":"Why should a report give history in chronological order?","answer":"so readers can follow how the site changed over time","explanation":"Reasoning.","explanation_ar":"ليتبع القارئ التغير عبر الزمن.","options":["so readers can follow how the site changed over time","so the report rhymes","so it is shorter","so it has more opinions"],"dok":2,"skill":"chronological order"},{"q":"A partner's conclusion lists three new history facts. What is the BEST advice?","answer":"Move the history facts to the body and make the conclusion about today and why to protect the site.","explanation":"Revising.","explanation_ar":"انقل الحقائق إلى المتن.","options":["Delete the introduction.","Add more facts to the conclusion.","Write it as a poem.","Move the history facts to the body and make the conclusion about today and why to protect the site."],"dok":3,"skill":"revising"},{"q":"Why do we protect World Heritage Sites?","answer":"They teach us about the past and belong to all people, so future generations can learn from them too.","explanation":"Purpose.","explanation_ar":"لأنها تعلّمنا الماضي وملك للأجيال.","options":["They are good places to build new roads.","They are only for tourists.","They teach us about the past and belong to all people, so future generations can learn from them too.","They make deserts greener."],"dok":3,"skill":"reasoning"}],"images":[{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS5-V01","prompt":"Picture for the word 'heritage': an old mud-brick palace with a Saudi flag"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS5-V02","prompt":"Picture for the word 'district': a map with one area highlighted"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS5-V03","prompt":"Picture for the word 'mud brick': workers stacking sun-dried bricks"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS5-V04","prompt":"Picture for the word 'restore': a worker repairing an old wall"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS5-V05","prompt":"Picture for the word 'rock art': carvings of camels on a rock"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS5-V06","prompt":"Picture for the word 'oasis': palm trees around a spring in the desert"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS5-V07","prompt":"Picture for the word 'World Heritage Site': the UNESCO World Heritage emblem over a site"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS5-01","prompt":"A student-made Heritage Trail booklet."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS5-02","prompt":"Four ancient-site fact files."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U1-LS5-03","prompt":"A mud-brick report planner."}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS5-V01","script":"heritage. Diriyah is part of our national heritage."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS5-V02","script":"district. At-Turaif is a historic district in Diriyah."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS5-V03","script":"mud brick. The palaces were built of mud brick."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS5-V04","script":"restore. Experts restored Salwa Palace."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS5-V05","script":"rock art. The rock art in Jubbah shows camels and people."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS5-V06","script":"oasis. Al-Ahsa is one of the largest oases in the world."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS5-V07","script":"World Heritage Site. At-Turaif became a World Heritage Site in 2010."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS5-T1","script":"**File A – Dadan (Dedan), AlUla** · Where: a green oasis valley near AlUla, northwest Saudi Arabia · When: center of the kingdoms of Dadan and Lihyan more than 2,500 years ago · Why important: traders on the incense route stopped here; inscriptions in the ancient Dadanitic script · Famous feature: the Lion Tombs – tombs cut high into a red sandstone cliff, some with lions carved beside them · Today: archaeologists are still digging here **File B – Rock Art of the Ha'il Region (Jubbah and Shuwaymis)** · Where: Ha'il region, northern Saudi Arabia · When: thousands of carvings made over about 10,000 years · What: pictures of people, camels, ibex and horses cut into rock · Amazing fact: Jubbah once stood beside an ancient lake, so people and animals lived where there is now desert · Today: UNESCO World Heritage Site (2015); protected by fences and guards from damage **File C – At-Turaif District, Diriyah** · Where: Diriyah, on the edge of Riyadh, beside Wadi Hanifah · Founded: 15th century · Importance: capital of the first Saudi state (18th century) · Buildings: Najdi-style mud-brick palaces with thick walls, small triangle-shaped openings and carved wooden doors that keep rooms cool; Salwa Palace = the largest, home of the ruling family · Event: attacked and largely destroyed in 1818 · Today: UNESCO World Heritage Site (2010); restored, with museums open to visitors **File D – Al-Ahsa Oasis** · Where: Eastern Province · What: one of the largest oases in the world, with more than 2.5 million date palms · Water: natural springs, canals and wells · History: people have lived here for thousands of years; the Jawatha Mosque is one of the oldest mosques in Arabia · Today: UNESCO World Heritage Site (2018)"},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U1-LS5-VID","script":"Lions carved in cliffs, camels drawn ten thousand years ago, palaces of mud… Which site will you report on? Choose a fact file: Dadan, the rock art of Ha'il, At-Turaif or Al-Ahsa Oasis. Imagine a city of golden mud-brick palaces rising beside a green valley… Paragraph 1: hook, what, where, when, why. Paragraph 2: history and features. Paragraph 3: today and why protect it! Paraphrase, keep the facts exact, credit your source – and add your page to the Heritage Trail!"}]}],"unit_test":[{"q":"Under the sand, the team found the ___ of an old mud-brick wall.","answer":"ruins","explanation":"Ruins.","explanation_ar":"أطلال.","options":["caravans","centuries","ruins","kingdoms"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"One thousand years is the same as ten ___.","answer":"centuries","explanation":"Century.","explanation_ar":"قرون.","options":["empires","inscriptions","settlements","centuries"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"A clay lamp, a bronze ring and an old coin from the dig went to the museum as ___.","answer":"artifacts","explanation":"Artifact.","explanation_ar":"قطع أثرية.","options":["trade routes","artifacts","monuments","caravans"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"The ___ of forty camels carried salt and dates from the oasis to the coast.","answer":"caravan","explanation":"Caravan.","explanation_ar":"القافلة.","options":["inscription","century","empire","caravan"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"UNESCO helps countries ___ important places so that future generations can see them.","answer":"preserve","explanation":"Preserve.","explanation_ar":"يحافظ على.","options":["preserve","decline","carve","ignore"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"The roots of *chronology* are *chron* (time) and *logy* (study). What does *chronology* mean?","answer":"the order in which events happened","explanation":"Roots.","explanation_ar":"الترتيب الزمني للأحداث.","options":["the study of rocks","a book written by hand","the order in which events happened","a group of one hundred people"],"dok":2,"skill":"roots","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"Which word means *a person who studies the earth and its rocks*?","answer":"geologist","explanation":"geo + logist.","explanation_ar":"عالم جيولوجيا.","options":["archaeologist","geologist","biologist","chronicle"],"dok":2,"skill":"roots","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"*The traders **established** a small market, and a century later it had become a busy town.* *Established* means ___.","answer":"started something that lasted","explanation":"Context clue.","explanation_ar":"أسّسوا.","options":["started something that lasted","destroyed","forgot about","carved into stone"],"dok":2,"skill":"vocabulary in context","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"Which detail must stay EXACT when you paraphrase *Hegra became a World Heritage Site in 2008*?","answer":"the year 2008","explanation":"Keep dates.","explanation_ar":"السنة 2008.","options":["the word *became*","the year 2008","the word order","the word *Site*"],"dok":1,"skill":"paraphrasing","_part":"Part D – Writing skills"},{"q":"Which sentence credits a source correctly?","answer":"According to the museum guide, the arrowhead is thousands of years old.","explanation":"Credit.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["According to the museum guide, the arrowhead is thousands of years old.","According the museum guide, the arrowhead is thousands of years old.","The arrowhead, according, is thousands of years old.","Museum guide according to, the arrowhead is old."],"dok":1,"skill":"crediting sources","_part":"Part D – Writing skills"},{"q":"Which is the BEST paraphrase of *Papyrus grows in the shallow water along the Nile*?","answer":"The papyrus plant grows where the Nile's water is not deep.","explanation":"Paraphrase.","explanation_ar":"أفضل إعادة صياغة.","options":["Papyrus grows in the shallow water along the Nile.","Papyrus grows in the deepest part of the sea.","I think papyrus is a beautiful plant.","The papyrus plant grows where the Nile's water is not deep."],"dok":2,"skill":"paraphrasing","_part":"Part D – Writing skills"},{"q":"Which time signal BEST completes the sentence? *___, the Romans took control of the Nabataean kingdom.*","answer":"In 106 CE","explanation":"Time signal.","explanation_ar":"In 106 CE.","options":["Once upon a time","As soon as","Finally,","In 106 CE"],"dok":2,"skill":"time signals","_part":"Part D – Writing skills"},{"q":"Which sentence belongs in the CONCLUSION of a report on Hegra?","answer":"Today, visitors from around the world come to Hegra, and protecting it keeps its story alive.","explanation":"Conclusion.","explanation_ar":"مكانها الخاتمة.","options":["Today, visitors from around the world come to Hegra, and protecting it keeps its story alive.","Hegra was built by the Nabataeans about two thousand years ago.","Caravans carried frankincense and myrrh.","Imagine giant rocks rising from the sand."],"dok":2,"skill":"organization","_part":"Part D – Writing skills"},{"q":"A classmate's report on Al-Ahsa is one 230-word paragraph. What is the BEST advice?","answer":"Divide it into an introduction, a body and a conclusion, and cut it to 150–200 words.","explanation":"Revising.","explanation_ar":"قسّمه إلى ثلاث فقرات واختصره.","options":["Add more facts to make it longer.","Remove all the dates.","Divide it into an introduction, a body and a conclusion, and cut it to 150–200 words.","Turn it into a poem."],"dok":3,"skill":"revising","_part":"Part D – Writing skills"}],"unit_writing":{"task":"Write a three-paragraph report (150–200 words) about an ancient site in Saudi Arabia or anywhere in the world that you know about – for example, Hegra, Tayma, Petra or the Great Pyramid of Giza. Paragraph 1: a hook and what, where and when. Paragraph 2: the history in time order and special features, with exact, paraphrased facts. Paragraph 3: the site today and why we should protect it.","model":"Which of the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World can you still visit today? Only one: the Great Pyramid of Giza, near Cairo in Egypt. It was built about 4,500 years ago as a tomb for a king named Khufu. (new paragraph) According to historians, the pyramid was made from about 2.3 million stone blocks. Workers cut the blocks, probably moved them on wooden sledges and pulled them up long ramps. When it was finished, it stood about 146 meters tall. For more than 3,800 years, it was the tallest structure built by people anywhere in the world. Inside, narrow passages lead to the king's burial chamber. (new paragraph) Today, millions of visitors come to see the Great Pyramid, and it is part of a UNESCO World Heritage Site. However, pollution and huge crowds can damage the ancient stones. Protecting the pyramid means protecting one of the greatest achievements of human history.","marks":20}});
